1
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:08,301
(Day Five, morning)

2
00:01:08,503 --> 00:01:12,339
This is an important announcement
from the Community Center.

3
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:13,373
"Important."

4
00:01:13,374 --> 00:01:14,374
My heart is racing.

5
00:01:14,742 --> 00:01:17,243
A new resident has arrived...

6
00:01:17,478 --> 00:01:19,746
outside the Community House.

7
00:01:19,981 --> 00:01:20,981
I got goosebumps.

8
00:01:21,683 --> 00:01:23,550
Oh, my gosh. What a surprise.

9
00:01:23,885 --> 00:01:25,485
I got chills.

10
00:01:25,887 --> 00:01:27,387
Are we getting an immigrant?

11
00:01:41,569 --> 00:01:42,502
It's a foreigner!

12
00:01:43,438 --> 00:01:46,140
A completely new person is joining us,

13
00:01:46,141 --> 00:01:47,708
and it's a foreigner!

14
00:01:47,709 --> 00:01:49,743
It was completely unexpected.

15
00:01:50,178 --> 00:01:51,444
It was a big surprise.

16
00:01:51,746 --> 00:01:53,413
A completely unexpected variable.

17
00:01:54,148 --> 00:01:57,250
I was doubtful that someone new could
come along,

18
00:01:57,251 --> 00:01:59,586
and that variable completely changed that.

19
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:02,123
Nations, customs, and borders are...

20
00:02:02,124 --> 00:02:03,890
really sensitive issues.

21
00:02:03,891 --> 00:02:05,792
Korea is a homogeneous country...

22
00:02:05,793 --> 00:02:08,495
and quite discriminatory
against immigrants.

23
00:02:08,496 --> 00:02:11,564
Of all the countries I've lived in,
I've never experienced...

24
00:02:11,565 --> 00:02:12,899
anywhere as racist as Korea.

25
00:02:13,534 --> 00:02:18,037
The new resident doesn't have
any personal funds to start with.

26
00:02:18,306 --> 00:02:20,540
Therefore, please discuss...

27
00:02:20,541 --> 00:02:23,310
the amount of settlement money
to give the new resident...

28
00:02:23,311 --> 00:02:27,382
with the two candidates
for Lifetime Leader.

29
00:02:27,383 --> 00:02:28,682
The settlement money.

30
00:02:28,983 --> 00:02:32,185
The two candidates have options...

31
00:02:32,186 --> 00:02:34,220
for the settlement payment.

32
00:02:34,221 --> 00:02:37,992
The current average amount
of personal funds per resident is...

33
00:02:37,993 --> 00:02:40,360
approximately 6,000 dollars.

34
00:02:41,796 --> 00:02:43,696
The first option is...

35
00:02:43,697 --> 00:02:45,966
for the six residents...

36
00:02:45,967 --> 00:02:50,437
who voted in favor of welcoming
immigrants in last night's discussion...

37
00:02:50,805 --> 00:02:54,775
to each contribute 1,000 dollars.

38
00:02:55,610 --> 00:02:57,377
The second option is...

39
00:02:57,612 --> 00:03:00,681
to deduct 6,000 dollars...

40
00:03:00,682 --> 00:03:02,350
from the community fund.

41
00:03:02,651 --> 00:03:04,030
- From the community fund.
- From the community fund.

42
00:03:06,554 --> 00:03:08,388
Once the payment method has been decided,

43
00:03:08,389 --> 00:03:10,957
the new resident will officially move in.

44
00:03:12,126 --> 00:03:15,628
This has been an announcement
from the Community Center.

45
00:03:15,864 --> 00:03:18,465
That's cruel.
She could be standing there for an hour...

46
00:03:18,466 --> 00:03:20,533
- if we don't decide fast.
- I know. We should be quick.

47
00:03:21,469 --> 00:03:22,569
And it's raining too.

48
00:03:31,712 --> 00:03:33,947
So, what exactly was the debate topic?

49
00:03:33,948 --> 00:03:34,747
Immigrants...

50
00:03:34,748 --> 00:03:37,950
"The media needs to stop portraying
Korean-Chinese people as criminals."

51
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:40,387
The announcement...

52
00:03:40,755 --> 00:03:42,989
didn't actually say "settle."

53
00:03:42,990 --> 00:03:45,359
"In favor of welcoming immigrants."

54
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:48,295
It said,
"in favor of welcoming immigrants."

55
00:03:48,296 --> 00:03:50,931
Six people were in favor of the media
stopping this kind of portrayal.

56
00:03:50,932 --> 00:03:52,532
It was 6 to 6.

57
00:03:52,533 --> 00:03:54,968
The first option is taking 1,000 dollars
from each of them.

58
00:03:55,136 --> 00:03:57,137
(Six people in favor: Polar Bear, Hippo)

59
00:03:57,138 --> 00:03:58,972
(Nangja, Go Ae Shin)

60
00:03:58,973 --> 00:04:01,441
(Gray, Michael)

61
00:04:01,442 --> 00:04:04,577
(First option: the 6 people contribute
to the settlement money)

62
00:04:04,578 --> 00:04:07,547
(Second option:
deducting from the community fund)

63
00:04:08,483 --> 00:04:11,651
The first option is to collect the money
from only those six people.

64
00:04:11,652 --> 00:04:14,221
The second option is to use
the community fund we've saved together.

65
00:04:14,222 --> 00:04:15,922
Isn't the second option more fair?

66
00:04:16,424 --> 00:04:17,958
That's up to the candidates.

67
00:04:17,959 --> 00:04:19,459
Do they get to decide?

68
00:04:19,861 --> 00:04:21,328
The candidates make the final call.

69
00:04:21,329 --> 00:04:23,363
I think we can discuss this all together.

70
00:04:23,364 --> 00:04:25,866
Neither of us is officially a leader,

71
00:04:25,867 --> 00:04:26,700
so we'd like to hear...

72
00:04:26,701 --> 00:04:29,903
I agree with Genie.

73
00:04:29,904 --> 00:04:32,039
We don't have much time,

74
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:36,143
and we're pretty united
and getting along really well right now.

75
00:04:36,144 --> 00:04:38,011
- Use the community fund?
- Yes. Use the community fund.

76
00:04:38,212 --> 00:04:40,280
- We can always rebuild the money later.
- I agree.

77
00:04:40,281 --> 00:04:42,415
I don't feel good about leaving
her standing outside like that.

78
00:04:42,750 --> 00:04:45,051
If we want to bring her in quickly,
we can just use the community fund.

79
00:04:45,052 --> 00:04:47,588
I think we should use the community fund.

80
00:04:47,589 --> 00:04:48,855
I agree,

81
00:04:48,856 --> 00:04:50,956
but I have one concern.

82
00:04:50,957 --> 00:04:53,093
The decision we make this time...

83
00:04:53,094 --> 00:04:55,729
shouldn't become the rule
for every situation from now on.

84
00:04:55,730 --> 00:04:58,465
They'll probably keep challenging
us with situations like this...

85
00:04:58,466 --> 00:05:00,200
for the rest of our stay here.

86
00:05:00,201 --> 00:05:02,936
They'll keep making us decide
whether to use our own money...

87
00:05:02,937 --> 00:05:04,437
or the community fund.

88
00:05:04,438 --> 00:05:05,438
Right.

89
00:05:05,439 --> 00:05:07,007
That's what it seems like.

90
00:05:07,008 --> 00:05:09,342
It's fine to use
the community fund this time,

91
00:05:09,343 --> 00:05:10,910
but we should establish some principles...

92
00:05:10,911 --> 00:05:12,779
for how we use it in the future.

93
00:05:13,114 --> 00:05:15,549
We shouldn't take
the community fund lightly.

94
00:05:15,750 --> 00:05:18,418
After giving her 6,000 dollars,
we'll only have 4,000 dollars left.

95
00:05:18,419 --> 00:05:20,321
- Right.
- I agree with you,

96
00:05:20,322 --> 00:05:21,989
but I'd just like to add one thing.

97
00:05:21,990 --> 00:05:26,059
This is just the first situation
we've faced.

98
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:28,295
I don't think we should jump ahead...

99
00:05:28,296 --> 00:05:30,663
and start worrying...

100
00:05:30,664 --> 00:05:34,568
that we're going to keep using
the community fund like this.

101
00:05:34,569 --> 00:05:37,036
They might keep making us use
the community fund.

102
00:05:37,037 --> 00:05:39,105
That's ultimately a choice
we have to make.

103
00:05:39,106 --> 00:05:41,007
"They'll keep making us spend
the community fund,"

104
00:05:41,008 --> 00:05:43,177
"so let's lay down some rules."

105
00:05:43,178 --> 00:05:45,945
If we start worrying about that now,

106
00:05:45,946 --> 00:05:48,648
it could plant a sense of crisis
in the back of our minds.

107
00:05:48,649 --> 00:05:50,850
When something actually happens,
we can talk about it again then.

108
00:05:50,851 --> 00:05:53,486
No. When I say we need principles,

109
00:05:53,622 --> 00:05:56,089
I mean we need to think
about how we're going to manage...

110
00:05:56,090 --> 00:05:59,793
our own money and the community fund
and what we do with it.

111
00:05:59,794 --> 00:06:01,595
If something comes up, I can say,

112
00:06:01,596 --> 00:06:03,263
"I'm sorry, but I'll have to collect
1,000 dollars from you."

113
00:06:03,264 --> 00:06:06,733
When another issue comes up, "I'm sorry,
but we need 2,000 more dollars."

114
00:06:06,734 --> 00:06:08,835
We can't keep going back
and forth like that.

115
00:06:08,836 --> 00:06:11,271
If we just spend money
whenever something urgent comes up,

116
00:06:11,272 --> 00:06:13,073
eventually nobody will have
any money left to take home.

117
00:06:13,240 --> 00:06:15,909
What I was saying is...

118
00:06:15,910 --> 00:06:17,510
"It's going to be this way from now on."

119
00:06:17,511 --> 00:06:19,712
"We'll need some kind of procedure."

120
00:06:19,713 --> 00:06:22,715
I just don't think we need to get
too deep into that discussion...

121
00:06:22,716 --> 00:06:24,317
like that right now.

122
00:06:24,318 --> 00:06:26,853
Can I add one more opinion?

123
00:06:26,854 --> 00:06:30,256
Let's first quickly hear if anyone is
against using the community fund.

124
00:06:30,257 --> 00:06:31,591
We can continue this discussion...

125
00:06:31,592 --> 00:06:33,092
after the immigrant comes in.

126
00:06:33,093 --> 00:06:35,062
Right. This is urgent,

127
00:06:35,063 --> 00:06:36,396
so let's use the community fund first.

128
00:06:36,397 --> 00:06:38,998
I'd like to add one more thing.

129
00:06:38,999 --> 00:06:41,468
I came here from the US,

130
00:06:41,469 --> 00:06:44,003
and Benjamin has ties
to both the US and Korea.

131
00:06:44,004 --> 00:06:46,372
When she comes in, I really hope...

132
00:06:46,741 --> 00:06:48,475
- we can welcome her...
- I totally agree.

133
00:06:48,476 --> 00:06:50,210
as warmly and comfortably as possible.

134
00:06:50,211 --> 00:06:51,945
It was raining heavily.

135
00:06:52,314 --> 00:06:55,883
She was literally trembling.
Anyone could tell she was a foreigner.

136
00:06:55,884 --> 00:06:59,987
People might just call me
a "Korean-looking foreigner."

137
00:07:00,221 --> 00:07:03,723
But for someone who looks
obviously foreign, it's much harder.

138
00:07:03,958 --> 00:07:05,325
I could call her a refugee.

139
00:07:05,326 --> 00:07:08,762
Honestly, she seemed more like a refugee.

140
00:07:08,763 --> 00:07:11,431
She had nothing
but an umbrella and a suitcase,

141
00:07:11,432 --> 00:07:13,533
and she didn't have a single penny.

142
00:07:13,534 --> 00:07:17,470
She reminded me more
of a refugee than an immigrant.

143
00:07:17,571 --> 00:07:20,239
- Let's go then.
- Let's let her in then.

144
00:07:20,541 --> 00:07:21,541
- Okay.
- Okay.

145
00:07:24,912 --> 00:07:26,045
It's so windy.

146
00:07:26,881 --> 00:07:30,217
Could we have more immigrants later?

147
00:07:30,218 --> 00:07:32,352
What if 50 refugees come one day?

148
00:07:33,622 --> 00:07:34,621
Look.

149
00:07:34,622 --> 00:07:36,356
It said the average personal fund is
about 6,000 dollars,

150
00:07:36,357 --> 00:07:37,925
which means some people have more...

151
00:07:37,926 --> 00:07:39,359
and some have less than that.

152
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:41,662
And she'll start with 6,000 dollars.

153
00:07:42,330 --> 00:07:44,164
I think that's where we might have...

154
00:07:44,165 --> 00:07:45,433
some differences of opinion.

155
00:07:45,434 --> 00:07:48,068
We've been working
and paying taxes all this time,

156
00:07:48,069 --> 00:07:49,303
while she's coming in as a new resident...

157
00:07:49,304 --> 00:07:50,470
and starting out
with more than some of us have.

158
00:07:50,471 --> 00:07:51,339
- Yes.
- Right.

159
00:07:51,340 --> 00:07:54,641
In a way, it's a form of welfare
for the immigrant.

160
00:07:54,809 --> 00:07:56,443
But the amount of support might be...

161
00:07:56,444 --> 00:07:58,578
- a little too generous.
- Please decide on the payment option.

162
00:07:59,413 --> 00:08:01,080
- Option 2.
- Option 2.

163
00:08:01,449 --> 00:08:03,984
The new resident's settlement money
has been decided.

164
00:08:06,754 --> 00:08:10,090
The new resident is now entering.

165
00:08:13,994 --> 00:08:17,462
Yesterday, I was pretty open-minded
about immigrants.

166
00:08:17,463 --> 00:08:20,866
But when I heard that 1,000 dollars
of my own money could be taken away,

167
00:08:20,867 --> 00:08:23,369
I realized I'd probably
react strongly to that.

168
00:08:23,370 --> 00:08:25,204
It's only natural to want...

169
00:08:25,205 --> 00:08:26,772
to protect your own money.

170
00:08:26,974 --> 00:08:29,075
We've spent
the last 4 or 5 days working...

171
00:08:29,076 --> 00:08:31,211
our butts off,

172
00:08:31,212 --> 00:08:34,080
putting in all that time and effort
to build up that community fund.

173
00:08:34,215 --> 00:08:35,982
So when someone suddenly showed up...

174
00:08:35,983 --> 00:08:38,784
and we just had
to hand over an amount equal...

175
00:08:38,785 --> 00:08:42,655
to the average amount everyone has,
that felt unfair to me.

176
00:08:43,157 --> 00:08:44,757
Six thousand dollars...

177
00:08:44,758 --> 00:08:46,326
is not pocket change.

178
00:08:46,727 --> 00:08:50,396
"Why should we trust her enough
to be this generous?"

179
00:08:50,397 --> 00:08:52,865
"Why do we just accept this
when it comes to an immigrant?"

180
00:08:52,866 --> 00:08:54,133
I wanted to raise some questions,

181
00:08:54,335 --> 00:08:55,835
but I thought they'd just be
suspicious of me.

182
00:08:55,836 --> 00:08:57,837
The way things are going right now,
it feels like a socialist system.

183
00:08:57,838 --> 00:09:00,140
If you ask, "Is this really okay?"
then you're a spy.

184
00:09:00,141 --> 00:09:02,976
In a situation like this,
nobody can really speak their mind.

185
00:09:02,977 --> 00:09:04,177
So I just kept my mouth shut.

186
00:09:05,312 --> 00:09:06,947
- Nice to meet you.
- Welcome.

187
00:09:06,948 --> 00:09:08,881
- Glad to meet you.
- How are you?

188
00:09:09,116 --> 00:09:10,917
Hello.

189
00:09:10,918 --> 00:09:12,485
Are you comfortable speaking Korean?

190
00:09:12,486 --> 00:09:13,519
- Yes.
- Your Korean is good.

191
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:15,221
Things might play out differently...

192
00:09:15,222 --> 00:09:16,156
than we expected.

193
00:09:16,157 --> 00:09:18,058
By the way,
our average balance is 6,000 dollars.

194
00:09:18,059 --> 00:09:19,992
Some people might have 4,000
or 5,000 dollars.

195
00:09:20,261 --> 00:09:21,995
We might not all be starting
on equal footing.

196
00:09:22,062 --> 00:09:23,662
It's like being born with money.

197
00:09:23,663 --> 00:09:25,297
We had no money when we moved in.

198
00:09:25,298 --> 00:09:26,132
You're right.

199
00:09:26,534 --> 00:09:28,235
We got past this situation for now,

200
00:09:28,260 --> 00:09:30,027
but now the real game begins.

201
00:09:32,373 --> 00:09:33,340
(Tensions Under the Roof)

202
00:09:33,341 --> 00:09:35,709
(The Community)

203
00:09:36,143 --> 00:09:36,909
(Freedom or Equality?)

204
00:09:36,910 --> 00:09:38,144
Thank you. Hello.

205
00:09:38,145 --> 00:09:39,979
We never even imagined.

206
00:09:40,475 --> 00:09:43,583
We did wonder if there might be...

207
00:09:43,584 --> 00:09:45,151
someone else joining us,

208
00:09:45,152 --> 00:09:46,486
and you showed up.

209
00:09:46,487 --> 00:09:47,754
You didn't know?

210
00:09:47,755 --> 00:09:50,056
No, we didn't. Nice to meet you.

211
00:09:50,057 --> 00:09:51,157
Nice to meet you.

212
00:09:51,158 --> 00:09:52,658
I'm glad to meet you.

213
00:09:52,893 --> 00:09:54,160
There are so many people here.

214
00:09:54,328 --> 00:09:56,263
- Her Korean is good.
- Her Korean is better than Michael's.

215
00:09:56,264 --> 00:09:57,196
I'm right here.

216
00:09:57,665 --> 00:09:59,599
- Her Korean is better.
- You speak Korean better than me.

217
00:10:00,134 --> 00:10:01,501
Where are you from?

218
00:10:01,502 --> 00:10:04,070
What do you think? Take a guess.

219
00:10:04,338 --> 00:10:05,305
Aren't you Korean?

220
00:10:06,874 --> 00:10:07,740
Maybe.

221
00:10:07,741 --> 00:10:08,775
India.

222
00:10:08,776 --> 00:10:09,609
No.

223
00:10:09,610 --> 00:10:11,378
Eastern Europe or Latin America.

224
00:10:11,379 --> 00:10:12,879
I'm from the Middle East.

225
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:13,880
- Arab?
- Israel?

226
00:10:13,881 --> 00:10:15,114
- Lebanon.
- Iran?

227
00:10:15,583 --> 00:10:16,750
- Iran?
- Yes.

228
00:10:18,853 --> 00:10:22,189
Hello. My name is Kimiya. I'm from Iran.

229
00:10:22,190 --> 00:10:25,892
I'm 23 years old. I came to Korea in 2017.

230
00:10:26,060 --> 00:10:27,560
Korean became really popular in Iran...

231
00:10:27,561 --> 00:10:30,764
because of old historical Korean dramas.

232
00:10:30,765 --> 00:10:35,001
I first came to Korea
because I wanted to learn Korean.

233
00:10:35,002 --> 00:10:37,937
Back in Iran, I attended a gifted school.

234
00:10:37,938 --> 00:10:41,875
I graduated early, received a scholarship,

235
00:10:42,176 --> 00:10:46,012
and studied AI Convergence
at Sungkyunkwan University.

236
00:10:46,380 --> 00:10:50,316
Banu is an elite international student
from a gifted school in Iran.

237
00:10:51,051 --> 00:10:53,453
She scored relatively low
across all the scales.

238
00:10:53,454 --> 00:10:55,087
She has a moderate political outlook.

239
00:10:55,723 --> 00:10:57,090
What does "Banu" mean?

240
00:10:57,892 --> 00:11:00,327
It's a Persian word,
and there's no Korean word for it.

241
00:11:00,328 --> 00:11:02,395
It basically means lady.

242
00:11:03,364 --> 00:11:05,030
What kind of food do you like?

243
00:11:05,099 --> 00:11:05,965
Food?

244
00:11:06,700 --> 00:11:08,768
I feel like we should treat her
to something tomorrow.

245
00:11:09,803 --> 00:11:11,938
I eat pretty much anything except pork.

246
00:11:11,939 --> 00:11:14,240
I was going to ask if you eat halal food.

247
00:11:14,775 --> 00:11:16,642
We can figure out...

248
00:11:16,643 --> 00:11:18,444
what to order together from now on.

249
00:11:20,381 --> 00:11:22,282
So, I have...

250
00:11:22,650 --> 00:11:25,285
- There's something I'd like to tell you.
- Okay.

251
00:11:25,453 --> 00:11:28,221
Did you find anything culturally difficult
to adjust to...

252
00:11:28,222 --> 00:11:29,489
when you first came to Korea?

253
00:11:29,490 --> 00:11:31,224
Not really.

254
00:11:31,926 --> 00:11:33,760
I'm pretty used to the culture now.

255
00:11:34,295 --> 00:11:36,963
Since you don't really know anything...

256
00:11:36,964 --> 00:11:39,332
- about us yet,
- Yes.

257
00:11:39,333 --> 00:11:41,001
I feel like I should explain something.

258
00:11:41,201 --> 00:11:43,903
You'll be receiving
some "settlement money."

259
00:11:44,038 --> 00:11:46,272
It will be 6,000 dollars.

260
00:11:46,273 --> 00:11:48,108
That money didn't come out of nowhere.

261
00:11:48,275 --> 00:11:51,644
I just wanted you to know
that it's from the money we'd saved up.

262
00:11:51,645 --> 00:11:52,445
Okay.

263
00:11:52,446 --> 00:11:53,980
We talked about it...

264
00:11:53,981 --> 00:11:55,748
and agreed among ourselves...

265
00:11:56,383 --> 00:11:59,619
that we wanted to use that to welcome you.

266
00:12:03,824 --> 00:12:05,625
And we're giving you...

267
00:12:05,626 --> 00:12:08,194
part of the money we saved together
as your settlement payment.

268
00:12:08,395 --> 00:12:11,097
I felt like I should explain that.

269
00:12:11,098 --> 00:12:12,699
Okay. I'll keep that in mind.

270
00:12:13,767 --> 00:12:15,434
Superman.

271
00:12:15,435 --> 00:12:18,170
I kind of got the feeling...

272
00:12:18,171 --> 00:12:19,572
that maybe he didn't really like me.

273
00:12:19,573 --> 00:12:21,373
Just from the way he looked at me.

274
00:12:21,609 --> 00:12:24,644
I felt like he wasn't too happy...

275
00:12:24,645 --> 00:12:26,845
about me getting the money.

276
00:12:26,846 --> 00:12:30,250
Maybe that's why he seemed
a little annoyed.

277
00:12:30,351 --> 00:12:34,620
He suddenly started telling
this new person,

278
00:12:34,755 --> 00:12:38,625
"I want you to know
that the money you got is..."

279
00:12:38,626 --> 00:12:41,694
"from the money
we worked really hard for."

280
00:12:41,695 --> 00:12:44,564
I thought that was kind of inappropriate.

281
00:12:44,565 --> 00:12:46,566
It wasn't even something she asked for...

282
00:12:46,567 --> 00:12:48,400
or chose to receive.

283
00:12:48,401 --> 00:12:50,369
She could easily feel like...

284
00:12:50,370 --> 00:12:53,873
there's some kind of power dynamic
between her and us because of the money.

285
00:12:54,675 --> 00:12:56,143
All right.

286
00:12:56,144 --> 00:12:59,178
It sounds like a lot has already happened.

287
00:12:59,179 --> 00:13:00,914
- Yes.
- I really want to be helpful...

288
00:13:00,915 --> 00:13:02,281
while I'm here.

289
00:13:02,716 --> 00:13:04,517
So please teach me.

290
00:13:04,518 --> 00:13:07,420
I don't want you to feel like...

291
00:13:07,421 --> 00:13:09,655
you have to be helpful.

292
00:13:09,656 --> 00:13:12,391
- I just want you to enjoy your time here.
- Okay.

293
00:13:12,392 --> 00:13:14,894
We're not exactly
the most helpful people either.

294
00:13:15,596 --> 00:13:17,664
She's one of the people
who contributed the most.

295
00:13:19,101 --> 00:13:22,802
Since you're here, I want to share
something for the first time.

296
00:13:23,270 --> 00:13:26,572
I actually spend
about half the year in Berlin.

297
00:13:26,874 --> 00:13:30,109
I go back and forth
between Berlin and Seoul.

298
00:13:30,277 --> 00:13:31,777
- That's cool.
- I know.

299
00:13:32,112 --> 00:13:33,112
That's impressive!

300
00:13:33,347 --> 00:13:36,082
We just found that out
about her after five days.

301
00:13:36,083 --> 00:13:37,650
Really?

302
00:13:37,651 --> 00:13:39,852
- Berlin has so many immigrants.
- That's true.

303
00:13:40,054 --> 00:13:41,588
- There are so many people...
- Right.

304
00:13:41,589 --> 00:13:42,789
from the Middle East, Africa,

305
00:13:42,790 --> 00:13:45,792
all over Europe,
just everywhere around there.

306
00:13:45,793 --> 00:13:48,595
Spending time in Berlin...

307
00:13:48,829 --> 00:13:50,797
really changed the way I see the world.

308
00:13:50,798 --> 00:13:52,899
- I can imagine.
- Completely.

309
00:13:52,900 --> 00:13:55,067
It really influenced me.

310
00:14:00,073 --> 00:14:02,942
(The announcement will now begin.)

311
00:14:03,444 --> 00:14:05,044
Will we get a mission right away?

312
00:14:05,045 --> 00:14:06,479
- All of a sudden?
- As soon as I get here?

313
00:14:07,013 --> 00:14:10,516
Lifetime Leader candidates,
Polar Bear and Superman,

314
00:14:11,018 --> 00:14:12,819
please submit one campaign promise each...

315
00:14:13,187 --> 00:14:15,688
for a new community rule...

316
00:14:15,689 --> 00:14:18,725
before tomorrow's election.

317
00:14:18,892 --> 00:14:19,792
A campaign promise?

318
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:21,694
A new community rule?

319
00:14:21,929 --> 00:14:24,264
- We'll be adding a new rule?
- Your promise can be...

320
00:14:24,598 --> 00:14:28,501
to change or remove an existing rule,

321
00:14:28,602 --> 00:14:31,571
or add a completely new one.

322
00:14:31,596 --> 00:14:32,495
Amazing!

323
00:14:33,640 --> 00:14:36,775
If a campaign promise is deemed
to seriously violate...

324
00:14:36,776 --> 00:14:38,844
the fundamental policies
of the Community House,

325
00:14:39,179 --> 00:14:41,247
it may be rejected.

326
00:14:42,349 --> 00:14:44,784
Once elected, the leader is free...

327
00:14:44,918 --> 00:14:47,920
to withdraw their campaign promise.

328
00:14:49,123 --> 00:14:50,890
- You can remove it.
- You can withdraw it.

329
00:14:51,158 --> 00:14:53,693
So they can make promises
they might not keep.

330
00:14:53,694 --> 00:14:55,461
Once both campaign promises
have been submitted,

331
00:14:55,863 --> 00:14:57,429
the two candidates...

332
00:14:57,698 --> 00:15:00,400
will take part in a face-to-face debate
after dinner.

333
00:15:03,370 --> 00:15:06,772
More details about the debate will be
announced later.

334
00:15:07,107 --> 00:15:10,409
Also, since today is the last day
of campaigning,

335
00:15:10,677 --> 00:15:12,511
the Reporter will get two chances...

336
00:15:12,512 --> 00:15:16,015
to verify information after the debate.

337
00:15:16,450 --> 00:15:20,119
This has been an announcement
from the Community Center.

338
00:15:20,120 --> 00:15:21,821
Today is a big day.

339
00:15:21,822 --> 00:15:23,823
- That's interesting.
- Today is going to be interesting.

340
00:15:24,491 --> 00:15:26,625
The clock is counting down.

341
00:15:27,093 --> 00:15:29,128
If I come up with a good proposal,
I'll make it my campaign promise.

342
00:15:29,129 --> 00:15:31,397
- All right.
- That sounds good.

343
00:15:32,166 --> 00:15:33,533
Is Room 13 the one at the very end?

344
00:15:33,534 --> 00:15:35,135
Yes. It's next to mine.

345
00:15:36,370 --> 00:15:37,971
I've been on this floor alone.

346
00:15:39,232 --> 00:15:40,799
I was wondering why.

347
00:15:41,753 --> 00:15:43,610
Then you can come out
once you get settled in.

348
00:15:43,611 --> 00:15:45,278
- See you later.
- Okay.

349
00:15:46,580 --> 00:15:50,483
(Banu's room)

350
00:15:57,557 --> 00:15:59,024
(Banu)

351
00:15:59,025 --> 00:16:00,192
That's how it looks.

352
00:16:02,629 --> 00:16:03,662
Four?

353
00:16:04,131 --> 00:16:05,331
Is that a score?

354
00:16:09,103 --> 00:16:13,339
I think of this place
as a whole new society.

355
00:16:13,340 --> 00:16:15,975
There are new rules
and a completely new culture.

356
00:16:15,976 --> 00:16:20,146
It kept bringing back memories
of when I first came to Korea...

357
00:16:20,414 --> 00:16:22,382
and had to figure out how to fit in.

358
00:16:27,554 --> 00:16:28,921
Right! Banu.

359
00:16:31,325 --> 00:16:32,325
Hello, Banu.

360
00:16:33,327 --> 00:16:35,295
Take Michael's spot, Banu.

361
00:16:38,098 --> 00:16:40,332
(Michael's room)

362
00:16:42,469 --> 00:16:43,436
(Banu, 4)

363
00:16:43,437 --> 00:16:45,538
She only has four points?

364
00:16:46,173 --> 00:16:47,640
She's the weakest now.

365
00:16:49,176 --> 00:16:51,710
(Benjamin's room)

366
00:16:51,711 --> 00:16:52,745
She's a weak link.

367
00:16:54,681 --> 00:16:56,448
She only has four points
and no immunity card.

368
00:16:57,017 --> 00:16:59,885
Goodness, that poor thing.

369
00:17:02,422 --> 00:17:04,757
Benjamin, do you have any ideas?

370
00:17:05,592 --> 00:17:08,027
- Let's brainstorm together.
- Together.

371
00:17:08,362 --> 00:17:12,799
It'd be great if we could each come up
with our own really good ideas,

372
00:17:13,133 --> 00:17:16,435
but Polar Bear and I are so different.

373
00:17:16,436 --> 00:17:18,104
Anyone can easily see that.

374
00:17:18,438 --> 00:17:20,239
I think we should come up...

375
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:21,607
with campaign promises together...

376
00:17:21,608 --> 00:17:23,542
that really reflect both of us.

377
00:17:23,543 --> 00:17:25,444
I don't think that'd be a bad idea.

378
00:17:25,612 --> 00:17:27,345
It shouldn't be about small things...

379
00:17:27,346 --> 00:17:29,548
that affect everyday life,
like immunity cards.

380
00:17:29,549 --> 00:17:32,051
That's not really what this is about.

381
00:17:32,052 --> 00:17:34,653
- It should be about our values.
- About what we believe in.

382
00:17:34,889 --> 00:17:36,789
Money is going to be a big issue.

383
00:17:36,790 --> 00:17:38,625
I think there's some resentment...

384
00:17:38,859 --> 00:17:40,493
because the immigrant received
6,000 dollars,

385
00:17:40,594 --> 00:17:42,414
while there are probably people here
who only have 2,000 dollars.

386
00:17:42,415 --> 00:17:44,397
That's why I had to tell her.

387
00:17:44,564 --> 00:17:47,032
Stuff like this will keep happening.

388
00:17:47,033 --> 00:17:48,333
If we keep making decisions...

389
00:17:48,334 --> 00:17:50,636
based on sympathy...

390
00:17:50,871 --> 00:17:52,004
or out of kindness,

391
00:17:52,005 --> 00:17:53,939
then what's the point
of playing a survival game?

392
00:17:53,940 --> 00:17:58,511
The one thing
you both care about the most.

393
00:17:58,512 --> 00:18:00,112
That no one gets eliminated.

394
00:18:00,113 --> 00:18:01,947
- "We don't want to get eliminated."
- Right.

395
00:18:02,617 --> 00:18:05,051
- A safe community.
- Security.

396
00:18:06,486 --> 00:18:08,020
In this election,

397
00:18:08,021 --> 00:18:10,522
people are going to vote
based on their own values.

398
00:18:10,523 --> 00:18:11,457
Also,

399
00:18:11,458 --> 00:18:13,592
if you look at all the topics
we've discussed so far,

400
00:18:13,593 --> 00:18:16,028
they've been about figuring out
what we value.

401
00:18:16,029 --> 00:18:17,563
- I know.
- Earlier,

402
00:18:17,798 --> 00:18:18,998
I just asked her.

403
00:18:18,999 --> 00:18:20,366
She said she doesn't eat pork.

404
00:18:20,367 --> 00:18:22,101
We ate so much pork on the first day.

405
00:18:22,102 --> 00:18:23,002
(A pork belly feast)

406
00:18:23,003 --> 00:18:23,903
Do you remember?

407
00:18:23,904 --> 00:18:26,606
I asked her
about cultural differences too.

408
00:18:26,607 --> 00:18:29,342
Did you find anything
culturally difficult to adjust to?

409
00:18:29,343 --> 00:18:31,644
In that sense, it's definitely harder...

410
00:18:31,645 --> 00:18:34,013
for Muslims to fit in with us.

411
00:18:35,716 --> 00:18:37,283
Because of that, I was...

412
00:18:37,284 --> 00:18:38,751
We all started with zero dollars,

413
00:18:39,019 --> 00:18:40,753
- I know.
- but she's starting with 6,000 dollars.

414
00:18:40,754 --> 00:18:42,388
We had to pay taxes
as soon as we got here.

415
00:18:42,389 --> 00:18:43,656
But the tax on her 6,000 dollars...

416
00:18:43,657 --> 00:18:45,725
is going to be taken out later tonight
when we all pay our taxes.

417
00:18:45,959 --> 00:18:48,928
That's something
I actually want to talk about.

418
00:18:49,729 --> 00:18:51,964
Safety shouldn't simply be...

419
00:18:52,132 --> 00:18:54,501
- That's the most basic thing.
- Right.

420
00:18:54,502 --> 00:18:56,435
When it comes to things
like taxes and money,

421
00:18:56,436 --> 00:18:59,271
some people really care
about them, and others don't.

422
00:19:00,173 --> 00:19:01,840
Just take your time and read through this.

423
00:19:02,043 --> 00:19:03,542
If there's anything you don't understand,

424
00:19:03,543 --> 00:19:06,512
- just ask me.
- I understand all of this,

425
00:19:06,513 --> 00:19:07,413
but I don't get this.

426
00:19:07,414 --> 00:19:10,115
- Did you see your own score?
- I did.

427
00:19:10,550 --> 00:19:13,018
If you figure out someone's beliefs...

428
00:19:13,620 --> 00:19:15,286
and their combination,

429
00:19:15,287 --> 00:19:16,789
you can eliminate them if you want.

430
00:19:20,094 --> 00:19:22,061
So is today the election?

431
00:19:22,062 --> 00:19:23,963
No, today's just the campaign.

432
00:19:24,131 --> 00:19:26,665
- Just the campaign.
- We vote tomorrow.

433
00:19:26,666 --> 00:19:30,536
Today, they debate and try to convince
everyone that they'll do a good job.

434
00:19:30,804 --> 00:19:33,372
I'll just give you the general idea
of what this election is about,

435
00:19:33,373 --> 00:19:36,041
since you don't really know them
that well.

436
00:19:37,144 --> 00:19:41,013
Both of them think it's important
for everyone to survive together.

437
00:19:41,038 --> 00:19:43,273
But Superman is more,
"Let's reward people who do well."

438
00:19:43,684 --> 00:19:45,751
Polar Bear is more, "Let's help
the weaker people out a little more."

439
00:19:46,020 --> 00:19:49,589
They've been really different
in that respect since the beginning.

440
00:19:50,023 --> 00:19:52,358
Now they happen to be the two candidates.

441
00:19:52,659 --> 00:19:53,859
The rest of us are...

442
00:19:53,860 --> 00:19:57,096
pretty much split 50-50
in terms of our views.

443
00:19:57,431 --> 00:19:59,565
This is going to be a really close vote.

444
00:20:00,434 --> 00:20:02,268
Up until now, whatever we did,

445
00:20:02,269 --> 00:20:03,836
most of our votes have been 6 to 6.

446
00:20:04,404 --> 00:20:06,271
(Day 1 Debate Results, For: 6, Against: 6)

447
00:20:06,272 --> 00:20:08,307
(Day 2 Debate Results, For: 6, Against: 6)

448
00:20:08,308 --> 00:20:09,875
(Day 3 Debate Results, For: 6, Against: 6)

449
00:20:09,876 --> 00:20:11,744
(Day 4 Debate Results, For: 6, Against: 6)

450
00:20:11,745 --> 00:20:13,612
(Lifetime Leader Support,
Polar Bear: 6, Superman: 6)

451
00:20:13,613 --> 00:20:16,181
Now that you're here,
and it's become 13 people,

452
00:20:16,416 --> 00:20:18,150
there probably won't be any more ties.

453
00:20:18,553 --> 00:20:20,753
Her stance is really important.

454
00:20:20,754 --> 00:20:24,190
Now everyone is going to be on edge...

455
00:20:24,424 --> 00:20:27,559
and start thinking
about what kind of politics...

456
00:20:27,560 --> 00:20:28,761
they want to see play out here.

457
00:20:28,762 --> 00:20:30,396
(She could be the deciding vote.)

458
00:20:30,597 --> 00:20:32,464
Does she have voting rights?

459
00:20:32,799 --> 00:20:33,732
I think so.

460
00:20:35,635 --> 00:20:38,203
She probably does because otherwise,

461
00:20:38,338 --> 00:20:39,538
we could end up with a tie.

462
00:20:39,706 --> 00:20:41,607
It could be 5 to 5, since we don't vote.

463
00:20:41,976 --> 00:20:43,542
That might be why they brought her in.

464
00:20:45,579 --> 00:20:46,412
Don't you think so?

465
00:20:46,680 --> 00:20:48,714
So that's how it happens?

466
00:20:49,583 --> 00:20:50,950
She just gets voting rights right away?

467
00:20:52,052 --> 00:20:53,753
You don't just give voting rights
to just anyone.

468
00:20:55,088 --> 00:20:56,555
This is basically a presidential election.

469
00:20:59,359 --> 00:21:01,460
How much each person gets to take home.

470
00:21:02,095 --> 00:21:02,928
That's decided.

471
00:21:02,929 --> 00:21:04,830
The leader decides that, so...

472
00:21:05,799 --> 00:21:07,600
Ted is so interesting.

473
00:21:08,301 --> 00:21:09,735
He's super smart and super nice.

474
00:21:10,570 --> 00:21:11,703
That's rare.

475
00:21:13,640 --> 00:21:14,773
Actually,

476
00:21:15,308 --> 00:21:17,843
there's one mystery
I still haven't figured out.

477
00:21:18,879 --> 00:21:19,846
Benjamin!

478
00:21:21,848 --> 00:21:23,015
His whole profile...

479
00:21:23,750 --> 00:21:25,350
is a lie.

480
00:21:26,419 --> 00:21:28,987
- What?
- His profile seems totally fake.

481
00:21:29,122 --> 00:21:29,988
Why?

482
00:21:30,227 --> 00:21:31,691
Well, it's not wrong,

483
00:21:32,392 --> 00:21:33,992
but I know him.

484
00:21:35,595 --> 00:21:43,202
(Polar Bear made guesses about Benjamin
on the first day.)

485
00:21:43,904 --> 00:21:45,271
Do you know "Good People?"

486
00:21:46,673 --> 00:21:50,643
It's a show about law firm interns,
and he was on it.

487
00:21:51,044 --> 00:21:54,446
He went to Seoul National University
for business and went to law school there.

488
00:21:55,782 --> 00:21:57,616
Did you go to college in the US then?

489
00:21:57,684 --> 00:21:59,918
I started college in the US
and then transferred.

490
00:22:00,253 --> 00:22:02,621
Did you go to a graduate school in Korea?

491
00:22:04,925 --> 00:22:05,892
He's a lawyer.

492
00:22:06,593 --> 00:22:08,093
But he doesn't say that at all.

493
00:22:08,495 --> 00:22:09,628
That's understandable.

494
00:22:09,763 --> 00:22:11,429
If you see it that way, I also didn't...

495
00:22:12,032 --> 00:22:13,165
Right. We didn't say everything.

496
00:22:14,935 --> 00:22:15,734
Still.

497
00:22:16,503 --> 00:22:17,903
What about Hippo?

498
00:22:19,439 --> 00:22:21,640
Hippo is kind of like someone
from the Justice Party.

499
00:22:22,442 --> 00:22:23,983
She'll call things out
when she thinks they're wrong,

500
00:22:24,378 --> 00:22:26,212
but she's also agreeable as well.

501
00:22:26,646 --> 00:22:29,214
You can tell she's so open-minded.

502
00:22:29,215 --> 00:22:33,118
She seems pretty politically correct.

503
00:22:34,321 --> 00:22:35,588
Seriously, though,

504
00:22:36,089 --> 00:22:37,523
I was personally upset...

505
00:22:38,024 --> 00:22:39,057
by what happened earlier.

506
00:22:39,926 --> 00:22:41,360
I'm upset that an immigrant came.

507
00:22:41,561 --> 00:22:42,561
Why?

508
00:22:43,230 --> 00:22:44,663
Since we had to spend our community fund.

509
00:22:44,664 --> 00:22:46,732
It's not about my money.

510
00:22:46,733 --> 00:22:48,020
"You feel bad
that she was standing outside?"

511
00:22:48,201 --> 00:22:50,302
"You don't feel bad
about people with no money here?"

512
00:22:50,303 --> 00:22:51,837
We have very different views.

513
00:22:51,838 --> 00:22:53,038
I thought the opposite.

514
00:22:53,039 --> 00:22:54,101
You let her know upfront.

515
00:22:54,102 --> 00:22:57,543
"We gave you some
of our hard-earned money."

516
00:22:57,544 --> 00:22:58,644
The way it came across to me was...

517
00:22:58,645 --> 00:23:00,679
by making a point of telling her that,

518
00:23:00,680 --> 00:23:03,015
you were kind of putting her
in a position...

519
00:23:03,016 --> 00:23:04,850
where she was supposed
to feel grateful to us.

520
00:23:04,851 --> 00:23:07,486
That really made me uncomfortable.

521
00:23:08,588 --> 00:23:10,956
But I really had to tell her.

522
00:23:11,324 --> 00:23:12,624
I couldn't have kept it to myself.

523
00:23:12,625 --> 00:23:14,059
But I just thought,

524
00:23:14,060 --> 00:23:15,861
"Why make someone
who just got here feel guilty..."

525
00:23:15,862 --> 00:23:17,496
"right off the bat?"

526
00:23:17,497 --> 00:23:18,797
- That's not it.
- That's what I thought.

527
00:23:20,033 --> 00:23:21,867
We really see things so differently.

528
00:23:21,868 --> 00:23:24,303
We're really
on opposite sides politically.

529
00:23:24,304 --> 00:23:25,971
I know. It's really interesting.

530
00:23:26,940 --> 00:23:29,508
It seems like I have strong views...

531
00:23:29,509 --> 00:23:31,277
on almost every issue.

532
00:23:32,088 --> 00:23:34,657
We're not trying to win votes...

533
00:23:34,682 --> 00:23:36,248
by turning people against each other.

534
00:23:36,249 --> 00:23:39,385
We can actually fight
based purely on our values.

535
00:23:39,386 --> 00:23:41,087
So honestly, I have no idea
how it's going to turn out.

536
00:23:47,527 --> 00:23:51,563
(Lifetime Leader Candidate
Pledge Registration)

537
00:23:56,803 --> 00:23:58,237
Making a new campaign promise...

538
00:23:59,072 --> 00:24:00,973
How do I come up
with a new campaign promise?

539
00:24:02,942 --> 00:24:08,780
(Polar Bear and Superman are trying
to come up with their campaign promises.)

540
00:24:15,422 --> 00:24:17,089
(The announcement will now begin.)

541
00:24:17,090 --> 00:24:20,159
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

542
00:24:20,727 --> 00:24:22,861
From now on, the two candidates will...

543
00:24:23,263 --> 00:24:25,965
each visit the Community Center...

544
00:24:25,966 --> 00:24:28,200
to see if their campaign promises...

545
00:24:28,201 --> 00:24:30,736
can be registered.
Then, they will submit them.

546
00:24:31,104 --> 00:24:33,872
(Lifetime Leader Candidate
Pledge Registration)

547
00:24:36,443 --> 00:24:40,079
(Polar Bear goes
to the Community Center first.)

548
00:24:40,413 --> 00:24:42,514
Please register your campaign pledge.

549
00:24:42,652 --> 00:24:44,049
My promise is,

550
00:24:44,351 --> 00:24:47,053
"I will return 100 percent
of the money the leader receives..."

551
00:24:47,054 --> 00:24:50,122
"from the community fund each day..."

552
00:24:50,123 --> 00:24:53,392
"and distribute that money to the person
with the lowest prize money..."

553
00:24:53,393 --> 00:24:56,762
"in order to build a safe community
where no one is left behind."

554
00:24:58,798 --> 00:25:01,233
If I become the leader,

555
00:25:01,468 --> 00:25:05,037
there could be a point
where you suddenly lose...

556
00:25:05,038 --> 00:25:07,273
all the security you've had...

557
00:25:07,274 --> 00:25:10,442
and find yourself at risk
of being eliminated.

558
00:25:10,443 --> 00:25:15,181
I don't want money to be the reason
someone ends up in that situation.

559
00:25:15,348 --> 00:25:19,151
We should at least have
some kind of financial safety net,

560
00:25:19,152 --> 00:25:22,688
and the leader should create...

561
00:25:22,689 --> 00:25:25,326
policies and systems that help people...

562
00:25:25,327 --> 00:25:28,294
get through personal hardships.

563
00:25:28,295 --> 00:25:29,628
That was my idea.

564
00:25:33,767 --> 00:25:35,901
Please register your campaign pledge.

565
00:25:35,902 --> 00:25:38,370
"I will set clear rules
for how prize money..."

566
00:25:38,671 --> 00:25:41,172
"and the community fund
are distributed and spent..."

567
00:25:41,674 --> 00:25:43,808
"so that people's hard work
is properly rewarded..."

568
00:25:44,243 --> 00:25:46,979
"and our shared money
isn't spent carelessly..."

569
00:25:46,980 --> 00:25:48,614
"in our community."

570
00:25:50,216 --> 00:25:53,718
There's no more
prize-money distribution...

571
00:25:53,987 --> 00:25:55,621
in the Community House.

572
00:25:56,856 --> 00:25:57,756
Is that so?

573
00:26:13,573 --> 00:26:16,642
I find it really interesting...

574
00:26:16,643 --> 00:26:18,310
how different Polar Bear and Superman are.

575
00:26:18,445 --> 00:26:19,912
They make
for such an interesting combination.

576
00:26:19,913 --> 00:26:21,080
And...

577
00:26:21,081 --> 00:26:23,816
- they have such different strengths...
- Right.

578
00:26:23,817 --> 00:26:25,251
that even I'm torn between them.

579
00:26:29,223 --> 00:26:31,624
We both managed to successfully register
our campaign pledges.

580
00:26:31,625 --> 00:26:33,225
- Good work.
- Great work.

581
00:26:33,226 --> 00:26:34,560
You must've had a hard time
figuring all that out.

582
00:26:34,561 --> 00:26:35,594
Thank you.

583
00:26:35,995 --> 00:26:37,162
Mine got rejected once.

584
00:26:37,230 --> 00:26:38,697
- Is that so?
- Really?

585
00:26:39,900 --> 00:26:42,934
It got rejected, and I looked at why.

586
00:26:43,202 --> 00:26:45,604
It said there wouldn't be
any more prize-money distribution.

587
00:26:47,640 --> 00:26:48,540
I have two guesses.

588
00:26:48,541 --> 00:26:50,675
One, no more economic activities
going forward.

589
00:26:50,844 --> 00:26:52,711
But realistically,
that wouldn't make sense.

590
00:26:52,712 --> 00:26:55,714
Second, you keep what you earn
without any distribution system.

591
00:26:55,715 --> 00:26:57,349
We might end up playing individually.

592
00:26:57,350 --> 00:26:59,084
It's highly likely
that we'll end up playing individually.

593
00:26:59,686 --> 00:27:01,220
We'll start taking money
from each other then.

594
00:27:01,454 --> 00:27:03,722
How we use the community fund is
going to become...

595
00:27:03,723 --> 00:27:05,591
a really important issue
for the next leader.

596
00:27:05,592 --> 00:27:07,993
So I decided to focus
my campaign promise on that.

597
00:27:08,396 --> 00:27:12,264
"I will establish clear rules
for how the community fund is used..."

598
00:27:12,833 --> 00:27:15,601
"through discussions with the residents,
so that people are rewarded..."

599
00:27:15,702 --> 00:27:18,871
"for their own efforts
and no one gets left behind."

600
00:27:22,041 --> 00:27:24,042
Your campaign promise has been registered.

601
00:27:26,346 --> 00:27:27,846
Polar Bear, did yours get rejected?

602
00:27:27,847 --> 00:27:29,447
Mine got approved right away.

603
00:27:30,016 --> 00:27:32,551
(The announcement will now begin.)

604
00:27:35,889 --> 00:27:39,024
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

605
00:27:40,059 --> 00:27:42,127
In addition to the powers
the current leader already has,

606
00:27:42,362 --> 00:27:44,996
we have some additional information...

607
00:27:45,524 --> 00:27:47,825
regarding the newly
selected Lifetime Leader.

608
00:27:49,969 --> 00:27:50,969
First,

609
00:27:51,437 --> 00:27:54,073
the Lifetime Leader is
the new person in power...

610
00:27:54,074 --> 00:27:55,840
in the Community House.

611
00:27:57,210 --> 00:28:00,612
During their term, they can influence
the residents' interests...

612
00:28:00,980 --> 00:28:04,449
throughout the remaining daytime schedule.

613
00:28:04,517 --> 00:28:06,151
Our only interest right now is money.

614
00:28:06,152 --> 00:28:07,419
Right, just money.

615
00:28:07,654 --> 00:28:08,621
Second,

616
00:28:08,955 --> 00:28:11,124
the Lifetime Leader will be...

617
00:28:11,125 --> 00:28:13,325
exempt from elimination...

618
00:28:13,326 --> 00:28:15,193
until the Community House's final day.

619
00:28:16,296 --> 00:28:17,296
Okay!

620
00:28:17,597 --> 00:28:20,399
The elimination immunity card
they already possess...

621
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:22,067
can be transferred.

622
00:28:22,068 --> 00:28:23,068
That's good.

623
00:28:23,770 --> 00:28:24,670
Third,

624
00:28:25,071 --> 00:28:28,774
the Lifetime Leader will receive
1,000 dollars per day...

625
00:28:29,042 --> 00:28:31,510
from the community fund.

626
00:28:31,878 --> 00:28:32,779
That's 1,000 dollars every day.

627
00:28:32,780 --> 00:28:34,379
If the community fund runs out of money,

628
00:28:34,814 --> 00:28:38,016
additional funds must be collected
to make the payment.

629
00:28:40,787 --> 00:28:44,690
This has been an announcement
from the Community Center.

630
00:28:45,858 --> 00:28:48,760
So there are definitely ways
for them to basically become a dictator.

631
00:28:48,761 --> 00:28:50,662
Being the leader sounds pretty great.

632
00:28:50,964 --> 00:28:52,065
There are only advantages.

633
00:28:52,066 --> 00:28:55,901
Soon, the two candidates will have
a face-to-face debate.

634
00:28:55,902 --> 00:28:57,068
"Debate instructions?"

635
00:28:57,069 --> 00:28:59,205
The two candidates will discuss...

636
00:28:59,206 --> 00:29:01,273
and choose a moderator.

637
00:29:03,276 --> 00:29:04,810
We already have a moderator.

638
00:29:04,811 --> 00:29:07,980
The designated moderator must visit
the Community Center...

639
00:29:07,981 --> 00:29:10,249
- I feel the pressure.
- and receive the instructions...

640
00:29:10,250 --> 00:29:11,717
for the debate.

641
00:29:12,185 --> 00:29:13,085
The moderator?

642
00:29:13,086 --> 00:29:14,552
What's funny is
that when it said "moderator,"

643
00:29:14,553 --> 00:29:15,854
everyone looked at him.

644
00:29:17,523 --> 00:29:19,023
Ted will be the moderator.

645
00:29:19,359 --> 00:29:21,994
Everyone somehow started seeing him
as a moderator.

646
00:29:22,295 --> 00:29:24,963
I'll moderate, but help me out...

647
00:29:24,964 --> 00:29:26,031
from time to time, okay?

648
00:29:26,032 --> 00:29:28,166
- I'm counting on you.
- I'll help you.

649
00:29:28,334 --> 00:29:30,035
Since you both agreed, I guess I'll go.

650
00:29:30,036 --> 00:29:31,736
- All right.
- Go ahead.

651
00:29:31,938 --> 00:29:33,805
Suddenly, I'm feeling the pressure.

652
00:29:34,040 --> 00:29:35,207
I'll do my best.

653
00:29:42,582 --> 00:29:45,751
Please receive the debate rules
and instruction cards.

654
00:29:46,853 --> 00:29:48,821
"Candidate Debate Rules."

655
00:29:49,456 --> 00:29:52,525
"Each candidate chooses a debate topic
that they think will work..."

656
00:29:52,526 --> 00:29:55,160
"in their favor and submits it
to the moderator."

657
00:29:55,561 --> 00:29:56,895
"Once they've submitted their topics,"

658
00:29:56,896 --> 00:29:59,264
"they get 30 minutes to prepare."

659
00:29:59,365 --> 00:30:00,432
They're looking out for themselves.

660
00:30:00,900 --> 00:30:03,168
Banu hasn't been living with us
for the past five days...

661
00:30:03,169 --> 00:30:05,403
and just showed up today.

662
00:30:05,404 --> 00:30:07,807
Does she get to vote as well?

663
00:30:07,808 --> 00:30:10,909
New residents also have voting rights.

664
00:30:10,910 --> 00:30:12,978
Okay. I got it.

665
00:30:13,112 --> 00:30:14,913
I'll go deliver this to them.

666
00:30:17,784 --> 00:30:19,184
This is interesting.

667
00:30:19,485 --> 00:30:20,752
Do we choose our own topics?

668
00:30:20,753 --> 00:30:22,353
A topic that will give you an advantage.

669
00:30:22,354 --> 00:30:24,189
Instead, you mustn't share the topics
with each other.

670
00:30:24,190 --> 00:30:25,423
So, we can't discuss it together.

671
00:30:25,424 --> 00:30:28,393
The topic should be something relevant
to our community.

672
00:30:28,394 --> 00:30:29,894
One more important thing.

673
00:30:29,895 --> 00:30:32,197
Does the new person get a vote?

674
00:30:32,331 --> 00:30:34,632
The answer was, "Of course."

675
00:30:34,867 --> 00:30:37,703
Honestly, I can't really accept that.

676
00:30:37,704 --> 00:30:41,640
The right to vote is one
of the last rights you give someone.

677
00:30:41,641 --> 00:30:43,642
But if we deny her the right to vote,

678
00:30:43,643 --> 00:30:46,245
it could look like we're excluding her
from the group.

679
00:30:46,246 --> 00:30:48,247
Winning Banu over could be
the game-changer.

680
00:30:51,818 --> 00:30:53,619
How about we cook?

681
00:30:53,620 --> 00:30:55,120
- Sounds great!
- I'm in!

682
00:30:56,689 --> 00:30:58,056
Cooking is fun.

683
00:30:58,825 --> 00:30:59,892
You're good at it.

684
00:31:00,460 --> 00:31:02,161
- I'll take this outside.
- Okay.

685
00:31:02,329 --> 00:31:05,430
We could give this chicken porridge
to Banu.

686
00:31:05,465 --> 00:31:08,434
Banu, these two dishes have pork in them.

687
00:31:08,435 --> 00:31:10,769
This is pan-fried tofu,
and this is chicken porridge.

688
00:31:10,971 --> 00:31:12,304
Serve yourself some rice.

689
00:31:12,305 --> 00:31:13,572
This chicken porridge is really good.

690
00:31:14,340 --> 00:31:15,674
It's been on my mind.

691
00:31:15,675 --> 00:31:18,643
I don't think we'll choose pork anymore,

692
00:31:18,644 --> 00:31:21,413
but we should probably be
a little more mindful of her.

693
00:31:21,414 --> 00:31:24,849
Otherwise, we might end up eating
all the good stuff ourselves...

694
00:31:24,850 --> 00:31:29,021
and leave her with very limited options.

695
00:31:29,022 --> 00:31:31,256
I know we can't really share
the same food,

696
00:31:31,257 --> 00:31:33,258
but some people just give...

697
00:31:33,259 --> 00:31:35,260
vegans some greens...

698
00:31:35,261 --> 00:31:36,928
while they eat
all the really good food themselves.

699
00:31:36,929 --> 00:31:38,863
They don't really think about making
or finding vegan food...

700
00:31:38,864 --> 00:31:40,165
that's actually good.

701
00:31:40,166 --> 00:31:42,701
They just think,
"Just give them some tofu or greens."

702
00:31:42,702 --> 00:31:44,803
A lot of people don't really think
beyond that.

703
00:31:44,804 --> 00:31:45,970
I think it's much better...

704
00:31:45,971 --> 00:31:47,939
if we all just have...

705
00:31:47,940 --> 00:31:50,275
a really good meal together without pork.

706
00:31:51,478 --> 00:31:53,512
- I'm still thinking about it.
- You are?

707
00:31:54,147 --> 00:31:56,515
I want to pick something
that has to do with the community.

708
00:31:56,682 --> 00:31:59,484
Doesn't Hippo know a lot
about community issues?

709
00:32:00,153 --> 00:32:01,653
Why don't you ask her?

710
00:32:01,654 --> 00:32:04,189
- Right. She's run a community before.
- Ask her for some advice.

711
00:32:04,190 --> 00:32:06,325
You know we have a debate coming up.

712
00:32:06,326 --> 00:32:08,727
I want to bring up an issue...

713
00:32:08,728 --> 00:32:10,662
relevant to our community.

714
00:32:10,663 --> 00:32:13,998
You know Superman kept mentioning
the community fund.

715
00:32:13,999 --> 00:32:16,835
I haven't decided on the topic,

716
00:32:17,036 --> 00:32:20,638
but I think I'll talk
about the community fund and taxes.

717
00:32:20,639 --> 00:32:23,642
It might be better to pick a topic...

718
00:32:23,643 --> 00:32:25,076
where he has a strong position.

719
00:32:25,077 --> 00:32:26,644
If people see him constantly pushing
his point...

720
00:32:26,645 --> 00:32:29,247
- Him being like that?
- and refusing to compromise,

721
00:32:29,482 --> 00:32:32,284
people might feel threatened by him.

722
00:32:33,120 --> 00:32:35,153
He might seem stubborn.

723
00:32:35,154 --> 00:32:37,623
People are going to pay attention
to something like that.

724
00:32:37,624 --> 00:32:39,124
I don't know
what Polar Bear is going to choose,

725
00:32:39,125 --> 00:32:41,192
but she might pick something
about gender quotas.

726
00:32:41,193 --> 00:32:43,496
A topic that really divides men and women.

727
00:32:43,497 --> 00:32:44,797
I also don't really like...

728
00:32:44,798 --> 00:32:47,733
the whole "brother" thing.

729
00:32:48,334 --> 00:32:51,636
Is it okay to call each other "brother"...

730
00:32:52,138 --> 00:32:54,473
in this community?

731
00:32:54,474 --> 00:32:56,942
That sounds more like a discussion
rather than a debate.

732
00:32:56,943 --> 00:32:57,776
Right.

733
00:32:58,177 --> 00:33:00,745
Superman, can you show me
your topic in a sentence?

734
00:33:00,746 --> 00:33:04,015
"What is a good way to collect
and spend the community fund?"

735
00:33:04,584 --> 00:33:05,751
Like this?

736
00:33:05,985 --> 00:33:08,019
One debate topic has been submitted.
Now I just need yours.

737
00:33:08,221 --> 00:33:11,123
Instead of "Everyone survives,"
what about "profit loss?"

738
00:33:11,290 --> 00:33:13,458
Some people might have a lot of money
but end up losing more money.

739
00:33:13,459 --> 00:33:14,659
- Okay.
- Okay.

740
00:33:28,875 --> 00:33:30,475
I really need your help, Sugar.

741
00:33:30,476 --> 00:33:32,211
I'm rooting for you.

742
00:33:32,212 --> 00:33:33,078
Thanks.

743
00:33:34,547 --> 00:33:36,582
Hippo, can we speak informally?

744
00:33:36,583 --> 00:33:37,983
- Shall we?
- Yes.

745
00:33:37,984 --> 00:33:38,817
Okay.

746
00:33:38,951 --> 00:33:40,985
It seems like a lot of people
speak informally with each other.

747
00:33:40,986 --> 00:33:42,820
I thought it'd be nice if we did that too.

748
00:33:43,056 --> 00:33:45,725
- Do whatever you want.
- Thank you.

749
00:33:46,259 --> 00:33:47,191
Please help me.

750
00:33:47,192 --> 00:33:51,029
I feel like a lot of people are
quietly rooting for you.

751
00:33:51,030 --> 00:33:52,631
I'd really appreciate your support.

752
00:33:52,938 --> 00:33:55,634
- Has the campaigning officially started?
- Ms. Polar Bear!

753
00:33:55,635 --> 00:33:56,468
Excuse me.

754
00:33:56,669 --> 00:33:58,636
- I'm rooting for you!
- I'll do the dishes.

755
00:34:00,606 --> 00:34:03,308
Everyone is so curious about you.

756
00:34:03,309 --> 00:34:04,043
What?

757
00:34:04,044 --> 00:34:06,411
People are getting more
and more curious about you.

758
00:34:07,213 --> 00:34:08,146
Benjamin.

759
00:34:08,614 --> 00:34:09,848
His whole profile is...

760
00:34:09,849 --> 00:34:11,382
a complete lie.

761
00:34:11,517 --> 00:34:12,584
He's a lawyer.

762
00:34:15,154 --> 00:34:17,122
You don't talk that much.

763
00:34:17,123 --> 00:34:18,189
About my background?

764
00:34:18,824 --> 00:34:20,258
What you're like.

765
00:34:22,128 --> 00:34:23,795
We feel like we don't really know you.

766
00:34:23,796 --> 00:34:25,030
But you always have...

767
00:34:25,263 --> 00:34:26,931
a big move up your sleeve.

768
00:34:41,180 --> 00:34:42,480
I...

769
00:34:43,182 --> 00:34:45,383
can promise everyone...

770
00:34:46,352 --> 00:34:49,020
(Superman is preparing for the debate.)

771
00:34:49,021 --> 00:34:52,223
I was a little upset today.

772
00:34:53,394 --> 00:34:55,727
(Polar Bear is also preparing.)

773
00:34:58,497 --> 00:35:01,265
The Lifetime Leader candidates' debate...

774
00:35:01,266 --> 00:35:03,234
will begin shortly.

775
00:35:03,536 --> 00:35:07,472
All residents,
please gather in the main hall.

776
00:35:15,014 --> 00:35:16,114
(Polar Bear, Superman)

777
00:35:16,115 --> 00:35:17,148
(Polar Bear, Superman)

778
00:35:17,149 --> 00:35:18,016
(Polar Bear)

779
00:35:18,017 --> 00:35:19,117
(Superman)

780
00:35:20,586 --> 00:35:22,787
Please go into the room.

781
00:35:25,524 --> 00:35:26,424
Goodness.

782
00:35:34,533 --> 00:35:35,666
(Polar Bear, Superman)

783
00:35:35,667 --> 00:35:37,101
What is all this?

784
00:35:39,739 --> 00:35:41,206
- Amazing.
- This is scary.

785
00:35:41,574 --> 00:35:42,774
I'm scared.

786
00:35:42,942 --> 00:35:44,476
You can open it.

787
00:35:44,910 --> 00:35:46,944
Have a seat wherever you're comfortable.

788
00:35:49,048 --> 00:35:50,448
Just sit wherever you want.

789
00:35:50,449 --> 00:35:52,650
It doesn't matter...

790
00:35:52,651 --> 00:35:54,252
which candidate you support.

791
00:36:05,998 --> 00:36:09,133
It looks like everyone has settled in,

792
00:36:09,134 --> 00:36:11,035
so let's get started with the debate.

793
00:36:11,703 --> 00:36:14,005
The Community: Tensions Under the Roof.

794
00:36:14,006 --> 00:36:16,508
A face-to-face debate
between the Lifetime Leader candidates...

795
00:36:16,509 --> 00:36:19,277
who'll be in charge of the community
for the rest of our stay.

796
00:36:19,278 --> 00:36:21,779
They'll be debating two topics.

797
00:36:22,148 --> 00:36:25,016
They'll spend an hour on each one.

798
00:36:25,017 --> 00:36:27,619
Here's Polar Bear's campaign promise.

799
00:36:27,920 --> 00:36:29,821
"The leader receives 1,000 dollars..."

800
00:36:29,822 --> 00:36:32,090
"from the community fund."

801
00:36:32,091 --> 00:36:35,827
"I'll distribute that money to the person
with the lowest prize money..."

802
00:36:35,828 --> 00:36:38,263
"in order to build a safe community..."

803
00:36:38,264 --> 00:36:40,434
"where no one is financially left behind."

804
00:36:40,435 --> 00:36:42,166
That's her campaign promise.

805
00:36:42,735 --> 00:36:43,835
Please keep that in mind.

806
00:36:43,836 --> 00:36:47,005
Now, I'll read
Superman's campaign promise.

807
00:36:47,006 --> 00:36:49,541
"I will establish clear rules
for how the community fund is used..."

808
00:36:49,542 --> 00:36:52,344
"through discussions with the residents,"

809
00:36:52,345 --> 00:36:55,213
"so that people are rewarded
for their own efforts..."

810
00:36:55,214 --> 00:36:59,250
"and no one gets left behind."

811
00:36:59,251 --> 00:37:01,219
That's his campaign promise.

812
00:37:01,854 --> 00:37:04,776
The first debate will now begin.

813
00:37:04,777 --> 00:37:05,824
(Debate Round 1)

814
00:37:06,092 --> 00:37:09,293
The first topic was submitted by Superman.

815
00:37:09,929 --> 00:37:12,063
"What is a good way..."

816
00:37:12,064 --> 00:37:15,000
"to collect and spend the community fund?"

817
00:37:15,935 --> 00:37:17,969
Let's get started with the debate.

818
00:37:18,571 --> 00:37:21,573
Something happened earlier today
that bothered me a little.

819
00:37:21,574 --> 00:37:24,176
When we were accepting
our new resident, Banu,

820
00:37:24,677 --> 00:37:27,079
I felt that the budget decision
was made too hastily.

821
00:37:27,080 --> 00:37:29,981
Personally, that didn't really align
with my principles,

822
00:37:29,982 --> 00:37:31,816
and it made me upset.

823
00:37:32,317 --> 00:37:36,153
We all have about 6,000 dollars right now.

824
00:37:36,588 --> 00:37:39,424
If that was actually money
you had borrowed,

825
00:37:39,425 --> 00:37:42,360
or cash you had saved up
and put right in front of you,

826
00:37:42,361 --> 00:37:44,362
would you have made the same decision?

827
00:37:44,363 --> 00:37:46,064
I'd like you to ask yourselves
that question.

828
00:37:46,298 --> 00:37:48,766
We don't know what kind of crisis
we're going to face...

829
00:37:48,767 --> 00:37:51,802
or what situations might require us
to spend the community fund in the future.

830
00:37:52,138 --> 00:37:54,272
We shouldn't make rushed decisions
about the community fund...

831
00:37:54,273 --> 00:37:57,476
just because we're caught up
in the moment...

832
00:37:57,776 --> 00:37:59,711
or because of the atmosphere.

833
00:37:59,712 --> 00:38:04,149
I can't shake the feeling
that we have been spending...

834
00:38:04,383 --> 00:38:07,585
our community fund almost like a game.

835
00:38:08,420 --> 00:38:10,388
We shouldn't look...

836
00:38:10,389 --> 00:38:13,391
at this large amount of money
as if it were just game money.

837
00:38:13,626 --> 00:38:15,293
We shouldn't spend it...

838
00:38:15,294 --> 00:38:16,995
whenever we feel good about something...

839
00:38:16,996 --> 00:38:18,663
or whenever we want to be generous.

840
00:38:18,664 --> 00:38:20,198
In that sense,

841
00:38:20,467 --> 00:38:23,401
there needs to be a reasonable process
for spending the community fund,

842
00:38:23,402 --> 00:38:25,837
and we need to be very strict
about how we use it.

843
00:38:26,005 --> 00:38:28,005
I will make sure we establish
those principles.

844
00:38:28,006 --> 00:38:30,776
I also agree that the leader
should use the community fund...

845
00:38:30,777 --> 00:38:33,878
based on what the residents agree on.

846
00:38:34,280 --> 00:38:37,415
What I don't really agree with is...

847
00:38:37,416 --> 00:38:40,051
saying that all the money
we've spent so far...

848
00:38:40,052 --> 00:38:41,319
was excessive.

849
00:38:41,587 --> 00:38:44,188
You said we were spending
the community fund...

850
00:38:44,189 --> 00:38:45,556
like it was a game.

851
00:38:45,557 --> 00:38:47,725
The leaders did listen
to everyone's opinions.

852
00:38:47,726 --> 00:38:49,660
Even when we ordered just one dish,

853
00:38:49,661 --> 00:38:52,697
we got everyone's approval first.

854
00:38:52,798 --> 00:38:55,199
I don't agree that,

855
00:38:55,434 --> 00:38:57,001
in most cases,

856
00:38:57,002 --> 00:38:59,705
the leaders were recklessly spending
money...

857
00:38:59,706 --> 00:39:01,006
based on their own judgment.

858
00:39:01,007 --> 00:39:03,074
Then let me ask you this.

859
00:39:03,442 --> 00:39:05,610
What if another new resident
shows up tomorrow,

860
00:39:05,611 --> 00:39:08,212
standing outside in the rain,

861
00:39:08,555 --> 00:39:10,948
and we need to spend 20,000 dollars?

862
00:39:11,450 --> 00:39:12,917
Would you make the decision
the way we did today?

863
00:39:16,956 --> 00:39:18,323
I think...

864
00:39:18,324 --> 00:39:21,225
today, the decision was made too quickly.

865
00:39:21,226 --> 00:39:24,597
It should go
through a proper discussion first.

866
00:39:24,598 --> 00:39:26,197
I can't answer that as an individual.

867
00:39:26,198 --> 00:39:28,900
"I'm willing to use
the community fund for this."

868
00:39:28,901 --> 00:39:30,170
I can't just say that.

869
00:39:30,171 --> 00:39:32,304
Saying what to do
about that situation now...

870
00:39:32,305 --> 00:39:34,039
would be abusing my power as the leader.

871
00:39:34,040 --> 00:39:36,141
It's pretty simple for me.

872
00:39:36,142 --> 00:39:38,510
If another new resident
showed up tomorrow,

873
00:39:38,511 --> 00:39:39,577
I wouldn't let them in.

874
00:39:40,246 --> 00:39:42,047
I can say that firmly.

875
00:39:43,382 --> 00:39:44,682
But let me be clear.

876
00:39:44,683 --> 00:39:45,817
She's part of our community now.

877
00:39:45,818 --> 00:39:47,618
I won't argue about that.

878
00:39:47,819 --> 00:39:51,455
Think about what could happen
if another person like her shows up.

879
00:39:51,556 --> 00:39:54,692
Just because someone happens to show up,

880
00:39:54,860 --> 00:39:57,462
our shared 6,000 dollars
could suddenly become 5,500 dollars.

881
00:39:57,463 --> 00:40:00,631
In a way, suddenly, this person becomes
financially better off than I am.

882
00:40:02,901 --> 00:40:06,337
I don't think I can justify
that to the community.

883
00:40:06,338 --> 00:40:07,805
I don't think I'd be able to defend that.

884
00:40:07,940 --> 00:40:09,207
I can say this firmly.

885
00:40:09,208 --> 00:40:10,709
If I become the leader,
whoever comes next,

886
00:40:10,710 --> 00:40:12,210
I won't let them in.

887
00:40:14,380 --> 00:40:16,881
I don't think the leader has...

888
00:40:16,882 --> 00:40:19,918
the authority to unilaterally decide...

889
00:40:19,919 --> 00:40:21,586
who should or shouldn't be accepted.

890
00:40:21,920 --> 00:40:23,654
That's why you saying,

891
00:40:23,655 --> 00:40:25,389
"I wouldn't let them in,"

892
00:40:25,390 --> 00:40:28,660
is basically the same as ignoring
the opinions of the members...

893
00:40:28,661 --> 00:40:31,161
who think that person should be accepted.

894
00:40:31,364 --> 00:40:32,897
How can you be so sure...

895
00:40:32,898 --> 00:40:35,633
that a new member would just be a drain...

896
00:40:35,634 --> 00:40:36,901
on our money?

897
00:40:38,671 --> 00:40:39,904
We can't ignore...

898
00:40:39,905 --> 00:40:42,507
the productivity or value
that a new member could bring...

899
00:40:42,508 --> 00:40:44,108
to our community.

900
00:40:44,109 --> 00:40:45,844
You're overlooking that...

901
00:40:45,845 --> 00:40:47,612
and focusing too heavily
on the initial cost.

902
00:40:47,613 --> 00:40:50,848
By using that as a reason
to completely reject someone...

903
00:40:50,849 --> 00:40:53,351
from the beginning,
you're really showing...

904
00:40:53,352 --> 00:40:54,919
how you're making the decision
unilaterally as the leader.

905
00:40:54,920 --> 00:40:57,555
Can we even guarantee
that a new immigrant isn't a spy?

906
00:41:00,793 --> 00:41:02,927
Think about how much time we spent...

907
00:41:02,928 --> 00:41:04,995
stressing out...

908
00:41:04,996 --> 00:41:06,530
just to win 1,000 or 2,000 dollars.

909
00:41:06,865 --> 00:41:08,933
This isn't money we can
just spend recklessly.

910
00:41:09,335 --> 00:41:12,704
So if everyone starts leaning
toward accepting an immigrant,

911
00:41:12,871 --> 00:41:14,138
and everyone agrees
that we should let them in,

912
00:41:14,139 --> 00:41:15,372
then obviously I can't stop that.

913
00:41:15,373 --> 00:41:16,874
I'd have to respect that decision.

914
00:41:17,176 --> 00:41:19,377
But if we're in a situation where
our money has to be spent right away,

915
00:41:19,645 --> 00:41:23,214
and there's a potential threat
to the safety and lives of our members,

916
00:41:23,215 --> 00:41:24,749
I won't take a huge chunk of our money...

917
00:41:24,750 --> 00:41:27,685
and spend it on bringing
another person into the community.

918
00:41:27,786 --> 00:41:30,454
That would be irresponsible of me
as a leader.

919
00:41:30,656 --> 00:41:33,091
A leader has to take the most cautious
approach when it comes to security.

920
00:41:33,092 --> 00:41:34,492
They should play it as safe as possible...

921
00:41:34,594 --> 00:41:36,261
when people's lives are at stake.

922
00:41:36,495 --> 00:41:39,063
You have to look at things
assuming there could be a risk.

923
00:41:39,264 --> 00:41:41,632
Your priority is to protect your money.

924
00:41:41,633 --> 00:41:44,468
And in that sense, I want to say
that I disagree with you.

925
00:41:44,744 --> 00:41:46,571
As for accepting immigrants,

926
00:41:46,972 --> 00:41:48,774
they can contribute to our community.

927
00:41:48,775 --> 00:41:50,575
I don't dismiss that possibility.

928
00:41:51,176 --> 00:41:54,511
But the proper process is to first look
at whether someone can contribute...

929
00:41:54,512 --> 00:41:57,248
to the community,
and then decide whether to accept them.

930
00:41:57,449 --> 00:41:59,016
Does that mean everyone has to prove...

931
00:41:59,017 --> 00:42:02,086
that they're useful to the community?

932
00:42:02,688 --> 00:42:04,288
If someone can't make money...

933
00:42:04,456 --> 00:42:05,756
or contribute economically,

934
00:42:05,757 --> 00:42:07,992
does that mean they can't live with us?

935
00:42:09,094 --> 00:42:10,561
I'd like to ask you that.

936
00:42:10,562 --> 00:42:11,929
Then, is the spy with us...

937
00:42:11,930 --> 00:42:13,531
because they're useful to us?

938
00:42:17,269 --> 00:42:19,270
Can we say they're useful?

939
00:42:19,838 --> 00:42:21,238
We don't know...

940
00:42:21,507 --> 00:42:24,876
exactly what role the spy plays
in the community.

941
00:42:24,877 --> 00:42:28,446
Neither of us even knows who the spy is.

942
00:42:28,447 --> 00:42:31,149
Talking about whether they're
useful or not...

943
00:42:31,150 --> 00:42:32,784
seems like the wrong question.

944
00:42:32,785 --> 00:42:34,652
That's what a resident should say.

945
00:42:34,653 --> 00:42:36,821
That's not the kind of response
we should hear from a leader.

946
00:42:36,822 --> 00:42:39,757
A spy could come into the community
and potentially threaten its safety.

947
00:42:39,758 --> 00:42:42,160
If a leader just welcomes
an outsider in and says,

948
00:42:42,161 --> 00:42:44,430
"Let's save a little more of our own money
and use it to help someone else,"

949
00:42:44,431 --> 00:42:46,031
that's not really the perspective
of a leader.

950
00:42:46,032 --> 00:42:48,333
That sounds more like an individual
in the community.

951
00:42:48,734 --> 00:42:50,467
This whole discussion is starting...

952
00:42:50,468 --> 00:42:51,969
from the assumption...

953
00:42:51,970 --> 00:42:54,405
- Okay. Fine.
- that someone new joins the community.

954
00:42:54,406 --> 00:42:56,207
This discussion seems like...

955
00:42:56,375 --> 00:42:57,476
it's based...

956
00:42:57,477 --> 00:42:59,243
on such an extreme example.

957
00:42:59,244 --> 00:43:00,211
All right.

958
00:43:00,212 --> 00:43:02,348
I think we should move on from this topic.

959
00:43:02,349 --> 00:43:04,115
Then let's not discuss...

960
00:43:04,116 --> 00:43:05,917
that topic anymore.

961
00:43:06,785 --> 00:43:09,387
I strongly agree with Superman.

962
00:43:09,388 --> 00:43:10,888
We're already struggling ourselves.

963
00:43:10,889 --> 00:43:13,624
If someone we don't know at all
suddenly comes along,

964
00:43:13,625 --> 00:43:15,326
we should take a conservative approach.

965
00:43:15,627 --> 00:43:17,761
If someone showed up
at Polar Bear's house, saying,

966
00:43:17,762 --> 00:43:20,464
"I'm starving. Please let me in."

967
00:43:20,465 --> 00:43:22,866
"Give me some money and feed me,"

968
00:43:22,867 --> 00:43:24,768
would she just let them in?

969
00:43:24,769 --> 00:43:27,071
There's no one in the real world...

970
00:43:27,072 --> 00:43:28,571
who would do that.

971
00:43:28,572 --> 00:43:29,974
That's just the reality.

972
00:43:30,075 --> 00:43:32,543
He said he wouldn't accept
another immigrant like Banu.

973
00:43:32,544 --> 00:43:35,613
I was worried
about how Banu would take that.

974
00:43:35,614 --> 00:43:37,714
I kind of took it personally.

975
00:43:37,715 --> 00:43:41,352
It bothered me, and I didn't feel good
about what he said.

976
00:43:41,820 --> 00:43:45,188
Superman didn't make...

977
00:43:45,189 --> 00:43:47,124
a great first impression on me.

978
00:43:47,125 --> 00:43:48,393
But after listening to him debate,

979
00:43:48,394 --> 00:43:52,196
I thought he made
pretty logical arguments.

980
00:43:52,197 --> 00:43:53,998
He didn't seem emotional either.

981
00:43:53,999 --> 00:43:57,568
I realized that he takes
these things seriously.

982
00:43:57,703 --> 00:44:00,038
I thought if he became the leader,

983
00:44:00,039 --> 00:44:03,107
he'd probably be pretty good at his job.

984
00:44:03,108 --> 00:44:04,709
As an immigrant,

985
00:44:04,710 --> 00:44:08,212
I might naturally prefer someone
more supportive of people like me.

986
00:44:08,213 --> 00:44:10,148
But honestly,

987
00:44:10,149 --> 00:44:12,150
Superman wasn't necessarily wrong either.

988
00:44:12,151 --> 00:44:16,020
If I were already a member
of this community,

989
00:44:16,021 --> 00:44:20,224
I might think the same way as Superman.

990
00:44:20,592 --> 00:44:22,927
When it comes to the community fund,

991
00:44:22,928 --> 00:44:25,796
I agreed more with Superman.

992
00:44:25,797 --> 00:44:27,698
All of us have worked for that money,

993
00:44:27,699 --> 00:44:31,001
so I was a little taken aback...

994
00:44:31,002 --> 00:44:33,637
by the idea of just giving it away.

995
00:44:33,805 --> 00:44:35,940
Anyway, just like I said in this debate,

996
00:44:35,941 --> 00:44:38,809
there's really one thing
I want to accomplish...

997
00:44:38,810 --> 00:44:40,144
as a leader.

998
00:44:40,145 --> 00:44:41,512
If our goal is...

999
00:44:41,647 --> 00:44:44,949
to work hard, make a lot of money,
and be able to take it home,

1000
00:44:44,950 --> 00:44:46,784
I'm willing to take on the hard work
to make that happen.

1001
00:44:47,219 --> 00:44:48,620
Things that ordinary residents may have...

1002
00:44:48,621 --> 00:44:50,154
a hard time saying or dealing with.

1003
00:44:50,155 --> 00:44:51,422
I'm willing to take those things
on myself.

1004
00:44:51,724 --> 00:44:52,657
More than just taking...

1005
00:44:52,658 --> 00:44:55,260
the pressure off them
to always look like the "good guy"...

1006
00:44:55,261 --> 00:44:57,195
or try to be generous,

1007
00:44:57,196 --> 00:45:00,431
I'll be the kind of leader
who fits this survival game.

1008
00:45:00,732 --> 00:45:02,566
That's where my promise comes from.

1009
00:45:02,868 --> 00:45:05,502
When it comes to collecting the funds
and deciding how they're spent,

1010
00:45:05,503 --> 00:45:08,639
I want to establish principles
that even the leader can't just disregard.

1011
00:45:08,640 --> 00:45:12,643
You said you want to establish principles
that even they can't simply violate.

1012
00:45:12,644 --> 00:45:14,178
So, what exactly are the principles...

1013
00:45:14,179 --> 00:45:16,514
you should follow
when it comes to spending the fund?

1014
00:45:16,515 --> 00:45:18,583
The area I would be willing
to spend the most money on is...

1015
00:45:18,584 --> 00:45:20,184
anything related to ensuring our safety.

1016
00:45:20,185 --> 00:45:22,353
I consider the spy to be a criminal.

1017
00:45:22,821 --> 00:45:25,289
I would use the community fund...

1018
00:45:25,290 --> 00:45:28,592
to identify and punish them.

1019
00:45:28,694 --> 00:45:32,397
There's no guarantee
we can identify the spy at once.

1020
00:45:32,398 --> 00:45:35,567
So, the way I understood
what you said is...

1021
00:45:35,568 --> 00:45:37,368
that you might use the fund multiple times
to try and find the spy.

1022
00:45:37,369 --> 00:45:38,770
Would the residents...

1023
00:45:38,771 --> 00:45:40,371
agree on that?

1024
00:45:40,372 --> 00:45:43,608
I'm not saying
I'll make this decision unilaterally.

1025
00:45:43,609 --> 00:45:45,910
I made it clear in my campaign promise...

1026
00:45:45,911 --> 00:45:48,846
that I'll establish those principles
together with the community.

1027
00:45:49,514 --> 00:45:52,918
Superman treated the spy like a criminal.

1028
00:45:52,919 --> 00:45:55,820
I think you have to put it like that,
even if it sounds extreme.

1029
00:45:55,821 --> 00:45:59,690
When it comes to dealing with
and getting rid of the criminal,

1030
00:45:59,691 --> 00:46:03,461
I found myself agreeing with him more.

1031
00:46:03,695 --> 00:46:06,897
"I won't hold back
when it comes to spending resources..."

1032
00:46:06,898 --> 00:46:08,232
"to keep you safe and eliminate the spy."

1033
00:46:08,233 --> 00:46:09,466
That got me thinking.

1034
00:46:09,668 --> 00:46:13,271
He said he'd be much stricter
about how we spend the community fund.

1035
00:46:13,939 --> 00:46:16,374
Then, he says he won't hesitate
to spend whatever it takes for safety.

1036
00:46:16,375 --> 00:46:17,875
Then, what exactly is his standard?

1037
00:46:18,010 --> 00:46:19,677
Will he be stingy with money
when we're accepting people,

1038
00:46:20,145 --> 00:46:21,746
but spend as much as we need
to track down the spy?

1039
00:46:21,913 --> 00:46:26,250
The person who was the most dismissive
about the whole spy issue...

1040
00:46:26,251 --> 00:46:29,119
is suddenly saying he wouldn't hesitate
to spend money...

1041
00:46:29,120 --> 00:46:30,320
to find them.

1042
00:46:30,321 --> 00:46:32,990
His words didn't seem
to match his actions.

1043
00:46:33,524 --> 00:46:36,794
Superman is clearly thinking a lot
about how systems should be designed.

1044
00:46:36,795 --> 00:46:38,668
He's fixated on the system
and seems like a smart person.

1045
00:46:38,696 --> 00:46:41,432
But I feel like there are some gaps
in his logic.

1046
00:46:41,433 --> 00:46:44,535
You need to establish whether the spy is
actually a criminal first.

1047
00:46:44,536 --> 00:46:47,070
But instead, he kind of jumps straight to,
"Well, a spy is a spy,"

1048
00:46:47,071 --> 00:46:49,206
"so it's okay to treat them
as a criminal."

1049
00:46:49,207 --> 00:46:51,975
I feel like he makes these big jumps
in his reasoning.

1050
00:46:52,044 --> 00:46:54,311
It's good that he thinks seriously
about how these systems should work,

1051
00:46:54,312 --> 00:46:57,382
but you need an expert to make sure...

1052
00:46:57,582 --> 00:46:59,783
there aren't any holes in the logic.

1053
00:46:59,784 --> 00:47:01,919
But I'm not sure
if he's actually an expert in that area.

1054
00:47:02,654 --> 00:47:05,089
It's great if our community makes
a lot of money...

1055
00:47:05,090 --> 00:47:07,458
and everyone maximizes their own gains.

1056
00:47:07,459 --> 00:47:09,260
But more than anything,

1057
00:47:09,261 --> 00:47:10,828
I just want all of us to be safe.

1058
00:47:11,630 --> 00:47:14,398
At the end of the day,
we have to be alive...

1059
00:47:14,399 --> 00:47:16,300
for our prize money
to actually mean something.

1060
00:47:16,301 --> 00:47:19,604
What if we end up with zero dollars,

1061
00:47:19,905 --> 00:47:21,739
and one day we suddenly receive
a rule saying...

1062
00:47:21,740 --> 00:47:23,741
that anyone
with zero dollars gets eliminated?

1063
00:47:23,976 --> 00:47:26,010
Would we really be able
to protect each other then?

1064
00:47:26,344 --> 00:47:28,946
That's why, as a leader,

1065
00:47:29,081 --> 00:47:30,548
I would give the 1,000 dollars...

1066
00:47:30,549 --> 00:47:32,850
to the resident who has the least money.

1067
00:47:32,851 --> 00:47:34,186
No matter who it is,

1068
00:47:34,187 --> 00:47:36,321
I want to make sure...

1069
00:47:36,322 --> 00:47:37,823
there's a safety net for people...

1070
00:47:37,824 --> 00:47:40,058
when they find themselves
in the worst possible situation.

1071
00:47:40,059 --> 00:47:42,427
I'm not going to give
you guys the money I receive.

1072
00:47:43,562 --> 00:47:45,897
I think of the salary
as my responsibility.

1073
00:47:45,898 --> 00:47:47,665
I'll see that money as a mandate...

1074
00:47:47,666 --> 00:47:50,434
to take on the responsibility
of building a community...

1075
00:47:50,569 --> 00:47:51,835
where we can earn 10,000 dollars.

1076
00:47:52,115 --> 00:47:55,006
That's why I'm not going
to return that 1,000 dollars.

1077
00:47:55,474 --> 00:47:58,176
I'm going to accept it
as a serious responsibility...

1078
00:47:58,177 --> 00:48:00,278
to protect you all and make sure
we survive until the end.

1079
00:48:01,613 --> 00:48:04,081
We'll take some questions now.

1080
00:48:04,082 --> 00:48:05,550
Does anyone have a question?

1081
00:48:05,551 --> 00:48:07,918
To whom and what would you like to ask?

1082
00:48:07,919 --> 00:48:10,654
Genie raised her hand first,
followed by Gray.

1083
00:48:10,655 --> 00:48:11,855
I'll pass you the mic.

1084
00:48:12,825 --> 00:48:16,761
Polar Bear, about you returning
the 1,000 dollars,

1085
00:48:16,762 --> 00:48:20,031
I'm not sure that necessarily guarantees
everyone's safety.

1086
00:48:20,032 --> 00:48:22,433
It costs money to do an ideology check.

1087
00:48:22,434 --> 00:48:24,001
If I were the spy...

1088
00:48:24,002 --> 00:48:25,403
and became greedy,

1089
00:48:25,404 --> 00:48:27,738
I could use the 1,000 dollars
to hurt someone.

1090
00:48:27,739 --> 00:48:30,708
Let's say one person has 5,500 dollars...

1091
00:48:30,709 --> 00:48:32,710
and another has 6,000 dollars.

1092
00:48:32,711 --> 00:48:35,546
Obviously,
the person with 5,500 dollars has less.

1093
00:48:35,847 --> 00:48:38,482
The person with 6,000 dollars worked hard
for that money.

1094
00:48:38,483 --> 00:48:40,417
Then one day this person
with 5,500 dollars would...

1095
00:48:40,418 --> 00:48:42,686
end up with 6,600 dollars overnight.

1096
00:48:42,687 --> 00:48:45,622
It makes me wonder
if that's actually fair.

1097
00:48:45,623 --> 00:48:48,592
I hope you'll reconsider that system
in that sense.

1098
00:48:50,128 --> 00:48:52,396
That's a pretty fair thing...

1099
00:48:52,397 --> 00:48:54,832
to be concerned about.

1100
00:48:54,833 --> 00:48:58,736
You mentioned that the 1,000 dollars could
be used in a bad way.

1101
00:48:58,737 --> 00:49:00,137
What I was really thinking about was...

1102
00:49:00,138 --> 00:49:02,741
the fact that people who don't have
much money...

1103
00:49:02,742 --> 00:49:05,309
might keep facing that kind of temptation
in the future...

1104
00:49:05,310 --> 00:49:06,877
other than the ideology check.

1105
00:49:06,878 --> 00:49:08,412
The whole point of my promise is...

1106
00:49:08,413 --> 00:49:10,013
to give people some money...

1107
00:49:10,014 --> 00:49:12,282
to help prevent situations...

1108
00:49:12,283 --> 00:49:14,284
where someone who's struggling...

1109
00:49:14,285 --> 00:49:16,386
might end up attacking someone else.

1110
00:49:16,387 --> 00:49:19,389
And about the whole 5,500 dollars
and 6,000 dollars,

1111
00:49:19,390 --> 00:49:22,427
honestly, I think I need to give
some more thought...

1112
00:49:22,428 --> 00:49:24,194
to how we should go about that.

1113
00:49:24,195 --> 00:49:25,629
There's something I want to point out.

1114
00:49:25,630 --> 00:49:27,331
Whenever we have these discussions,

1115
00:49:27,566 --> 00:49:30,134
we always imagine ourselves
as the person with more money.

1116
00:49:30,135 --> 00:49:32,303
We judge whether something is fair...

1117
00:49:32,304 --> 00:49:35,506
by imagining that we're the person
with 6,000 dollars.

1118
00:49:36,074 --> 00:49:37,341
Where you stand in life,

1119
00:49:37,342 --> 00:49:39,443
financially and otherwise,

1120
00:49:39,644 --> 00:49:41,311
can change at any time.

1121
00:49:41,513 --> 00:49:43,914
I proposed it because I thought...

1122
00:49:43,915 --> 00:49:46,150
we needed some kind of system...

1123
00:49:46,151 --> 00:49:47,485
to help guarantee our safety.

1124
00:49:47,986 --> 00:49:49,920
Although a thousand dollars might not be
a huge amount of money,

1125
00:49:49,921 --> 00:49:52,923
she would actually spend that money...

1126
00:49:52,924 --> 00:49:54,925
for the sake of the community.

1127
00:49:54,926 --> 00:49:58,095
It gave me confidence in her values.

1128
00:49:58,096 --> 00:49:59,730
Her words and actions actually match.

1129
00:50:00,031 --> 00:50:04,401
Polar Bear created the donation policy
with good intentions.

1130
00:50:04,402 --> 00:50:06,336
But the policy is too loosely designed...

1131
00:50:06,471 --> 00:50:08,238
and maybe a little too idealistic.

1132
00:50:08,239 --> 00:50:10,340
That policy comes
from a really good place,

1133
00:50:10,609 --> 00:50:12,543
but I didn't really see her explain...

1134
00:50:12,544 --> 00:50:15,079
what she would actually do...

1135
00:50:15,347 --> 00:50:17,682
when things get difficult...

1136
00:50:17,683 --> 00:50:20,918
and we're faced with a situation...

1137
00:50:20,919 --> 00:50:23,688
where good intentions alone aren't enough.

1138
00:50:23,689 --> 00:50:27,191
In that sense,
I think Superman is much more realistic.

1139
00:50:27,759 --> 00:50:30,260
Because the real world doesn't
work that way.

1140
00:50:31,029 --> 00:50:32,363
Superman,

1141
00:50:32,364 --> 00:50:36,033
you said you'd treat
the 1,000 dollars you receive...

1142
00:50:36,034 --> 00:50:37,534
as your salary...

1143
00:50:37,770 --> 00:50:40,104
and focus on helping us make...

1144
00:50:40,105 --> 00:50:41,839
more than 10,000 dollars.

1145
00:50:42,107 --> 00:50:44,608
But if there are
no more income activities,

1146
00:50:44,810 --> 00:50:47,111
some people might be unhappy...

1147
00:50:47,112 --> 00:50:48,145
about that,

1148
00:50:48,146 --> 00:50:51,115
given the other candidate promises
to return the money.

1149
00:50:51,383 --> 00:50:53,150
If there's no economic activity,

1150
00:50:53,151 --> 00:50:55,886
would you be willing to take
what you said back...

1151
00:50:56,154 --> 00:50:58,856
and give your 1,000 dollars back
to the community?

1152
00:50:59,391 --> 00:51:02,025
No. No matter what the situation is,

1153
00:51:02,026 --> 00:51:03,593
I don't see the 1,000 dollars I'm given...

1154
00:51:03,594 --> 00:51:06,129
as something that I can just hand out
to people to look generous.

1155
00:51:06,264 --> 00:51:09,700
If I said I'd return the money
in any situation,

1156
00:51:09,834 --> 00:51:11,367
that would feel like I'm neglecting
my responsibility...

1157
00:51:11,368 --> 00:51:13,069
and covering up my own incompetence...

1158
00:51:13,070 --> 00:51:14,371
by giving that money away instead.

1159
00:51:14,372 --> 00:51:16,039
That's hard for me to accept.

1160
00:51:16,040 --> 00:51:18,275
It's not like I really want
that 1,000 dollars.

1161
00:51:18,276 --> 00:51:21,210
There are some things she said earlier...

1162
00:51:21,211 --> 00:51:22,613
that I see differently.

1163
00:51:22,614 --> 00:51:25,782
My priority is to do everything I can
to make sure...

1164
00:51:25,783 --> 00:51:27,417
that 6,000 dollars doesn't turn
into 3,000 dollars.

1165
00:51:27,418 --> 00:51:29,619
I'll make sure you don't have to worry...

1166
00:51:29,620 --> 00:51:31,655
about ending up with zero dollars.

1167
00:51:32,157 --> 00:51:33,891
If you have 5,000 dollars,

1168
00:51:34,125 --> 00:51:35,459
you should be able to trust...

1169
00:51:35,460 --> 00:51:38,195
that you'll protect most of that money
no matter how hard the mission is.

1170
00:51:38,196 --> 00:51:41,098
A leader's job is to give you
that sense of security.

1171
00:51:41,099 --> 00:51:43,233
"You never know when you might
end up with zero dollars,"

1172
00:51:43,234 --> 00:51:45,135
"so I'll give you 1,000 dollars
to prepare for that."

1173
00:51:45,136 --> 00:51:46,436
That's not my approach at all.

1174
00:51:46,437 --> 00:51:51,308
When I say I'd return the money
to the community,

1175
00:51:51,876 --> 00:51:54,845
I don't mean I'll neglect
my responsibility.

1176
00:51:54,846 --> 00:51:58,181
It comes from wanting
to build a community...

1177
00:51:58,182 --> 00:51:59,649
where we can be a little safer.

1178
00:51:59,650 --> 00:52:01,585
But there was one thing he said...

1179
00:52:01,586 --> 00:52:03,954
that I had a hard time understanding.

1180
00:52:03,955 --> 00:52:06,289
He said that as a leader, he'd make sure
we could make a lot of money,

1181
00:52:06,290 --> 00:52:08,826
which could end up being
a promise he can't keep.

1182
00:52:08,827 --> 00:52:10,694
We've already done the income activities.

1183
00:52:10,861 --> 00:52:13,430
Was it something we could control
just by trying hard?

1184
00:52:13,431 --> 00:52:15,465
I've only made promises
that I know I can keep,

1185
00:52:15,466 --> 00:52:18,502
and I want to say once again
that I will make sure I keep them.

1186
00:52:19,771 --> 00:52:22,039
Thank you. That was our first topic,

1187
00:52:22,040 --> 00:52:24,742
"What is a good way to collect
and spend the community fund?"

1188
00:52:24,743 --> 00:52:27,277
That wraps up our first topic.

1189
00:52:27,278 --> 00:52:29,813
Let's give our debaters
a round of applause.

1190
00:52:32,584 --> 00:52:35,119
The first debate has ended.

1191
00:52:35,420 --> 00:52:37,355
- They're so good.
- They are.

1192
00:52:37,356 --> 00:52:40,024
- I'm impressed.
- They're really good speakers.

1193
00:52:40,324 --> 00:52:42,226
I'm glad I'm not a candidate.

1194
00:52:43,261 --> 00:52:45,863
- I don't even want to imagine.
- You would've done well.

1195
00:52:46,331 --> 00:52:48,198
I've realized something from being here.

1196
00:52:48,533 --> 00:52:51,468
The person I am in real life is
different from who I am here.

1197
00:52:51,536 --> 00:52:53,537
In real life, I already have a career,

1198
00:52:53,538 --> 00:52:54,738
but here, I don't even have a job.

1199
00:52:54,739 --> 00:52:57,574
I just happen to have some money
in my pocket.

1200
00:52:57,575 --> 00:52:58,876
Depending on how much money I have,

1201
00:52:58,877 --> 00:53:00,377
I could end up being below average...

1202
00:53:00,378 --> 00:53:01,948
or above average.

1203
00:53:01,949 --> 00:53:03,347
And that changes the way I see things.

1204
00:53:03,748 --> 00:53:04,781
I had the same thought.

1205
00:53:04,983 --> 00:53:07,184
It's so different from the way
I've always seen things.

1206
00:53:07,185 --> 00:53:09,486
You can really look at things differently.

1207
00:53:09,487 --> 00:53:11,555
And that's directly connected...

1208
00:53:11,556 --> 00:53:12,656
- to my life.
- Our survival.

1209
00:53:12,657 --> 00:53:14,124
Our lives and our survival.

1210
00:53:14,325 --> 00:53:17,360
In my normal life,
I haven't really had many chances...

1211
00:53:17,595 --> 00:53:20,265
to think about people who are
in a different position from me.

1212
00:53:20,266 --> 00:53:22,666
But here, I could end up living
that opposite kind of life.

1213
00:53:22,667 --> 00:53:23,500
Exactly.

1214
00:53:23,836 --> 00:53:24,869
At least in here.

1215
00:53:25,371 --> 00:53:27,504
That's really fun...

1216
00:53:27,739 --> 00:53:29,273
- and cool.
- I know!

1217
00:53:30,008 --> 00:53:32,643
(Lifetime Leader Debate Round 2)

1218
00:53:32,644 --> 00:53:33,946
"If everyone has to give up..."

1219
00:53:33,947 --> 00:53:36,184
"part of our prize money
to make sure everyone survives,"

1220
00:53:36,185 --> 00:53:38,382
"what should the leader do?"

1221
00:53:38,383 --> 00:53:39,750
"Should they respect
everyone's individual freedom?"

1222
00:53:39,751 --> 00:53:42,019
"Or should they make sure everyone
in the community survives?"

1223
00:53:42,387 --> 00:53:44,188
"What kind of philosophy should
the leader have?"

1224
00:53:44,189 --> 00:53:45,556
If we don't survive,

1225
00:53:45,657 --> 00:53:48,258
none of us gets to take
our prize money home anyway.

1226
00:53:48,259 --> 00:53:49,627
I'd choose not to leave anyone behind.

1227
00:53:49,628 --> 00:53:52,396
Even if it means we lose some money
along the way,

1228
00:53:52,397 --> 00:53:55,199
my priority would always be...

1229
00:53:55,200 --> 00:53:58,168
making sure everyone makes it to the end.

1230
00:53:58,169 --> 00:54:01,672
I'm more of a person who thinks
the individual matters more.

1231
00:54:01,673 --> 00:54:03,874
I think it's dangerous...

1232
00:54:03,875 --> 00:54:05,509
to limit people's freedom...

1233
00:54:05,510 --> 00:54:08,312
just for the sake of keeping
the whole community safe.

1234
00:54:08,513 --> 00:54:10,681
I'm not going to put this pressure
on the residents...

1235
00:54:10,882 --> 00:54:13,250
to always do the right thing or be nice...

1236
00:54:13,437 --> 00:54:15,185
just because of this abstract idea
of keeping the community safe.

1237
00:54:15,186 --> 00:54:17,487
I'd rather take
on those uncomfortable decisions myself.

1238
00:54:17,789 --> 00:54:20,023
You've been putting a lot of emphasis...

1239
00:54:20,024 --> 00:54:23,126
on making sure each person gets
the most benefit possible.

1240
00:54:23,294 --> 00:54:25,662
Does that mean you'll make sure...

1241
00:54:25,663 --> 00:54:27,665
each individual gets the most benefit,

1242
00:54:27,666 --> 00:54:29,532
even if it means eliminating some people?

1243
00:54:29,734 --> 00:54:32,002
A leader has a responsibility...

1244
00:54:32,003 --> 00:54:34,772
to take the most cautious approach
when it comes to protecting...

1245
00:54:34,773 --> 00:54:37,040
the community's safety, lives,
and property.

1246
00:54:37,041 --> 00:54:38,875
Let me give you a hypothetical situation.

1247
00:54:38,876 --> 00:54:41,078
You must eliminate one of three residents.

1248
00:54:41,079 --> 00:54:42,312
Let me ask you a question.

1249
00:54:42,313 --> 00:54:44,114
You have to eliminate one.

1250
00:54:44,115 --> 00:54:45,882
One person has 5,000 dollars,
another has 3,000 dollars,

1251
00:54:45,883 --> 00:54:46,950
and the third has 1,000 dollars.

1252
00:54:46,951 --> 00:54:48,919
Whoever gets eliminated,
their money goes back to the community.

1253
00:54:48,920 --> 00:54:50,020
Which one would you eliminate?

1254
00:54:50,388 --> 00:54:51,588
You have to eliminate one.

1255
00:54:52,056 --> 00:54:53,257
I'd choose not to kill anyone.

1256
00:54:53,258 --> 00:54:54,724
What if you absolutely had
to eliminate someone?

1257
00:54:54,725 --> 00:54:56,226
You're giving
a really extreme example here.

1258
00:54:56,227 --> 00:54:59,029
You can't take
a situation like that so lightly.

1259
00:54:59,030 --> 00:55:00,731
So if you had to choose,
who would you pick?

1260
00:55:00,732 --> 00:55:02,065
Obviously,
I'd pick the person with 1,000 dollars.

1261
00:55:02,433 --> 00:55:03,600
What about you?

1262
00:55:03,868 --> 00:55:05,037
Why is that obvious?

1263
00:55:05,038 --> 00:55:06,336
It's what's best for the community.

1264
00:55:06,705 --> 00:55:10,006
What I'm saying is that we need
to be prepared for situations like this.

1265
00:55:10,007 --> 00:55:13,043
I get what you're saying.
I understand your point.

1266
00:55:13,878 --> 00:55:16,279
But I'm not trying
to decide who is better.

1267
00:55:16,848 --> 00:55:19,349
I'm saying I'd use whatever power
and authority I have...

1268
00:55:19,350 --> 00:55:21,954
to make sure nobody gets eliminated
in the first place.

1269
00:55:21,955 --> 00:55:24,788
And even if someone does get eliminated,
I'd do everything I can to protect them.

1270
00:55:24,789 --> 00:55:26,390
That's just hypocritical.

1271
00:55:26,391 --> 00:55:27,491
How is that hypocritical?

1272
00:55:27,492 --> 00:55:29,493
Honestly, even calling
this hypocrisy is...

1273
00:55:29,494 --> 00:55:31,562
pretty subjective.

1274
00:55:31,563 --> 00:55:33,463
- That's just pretentious.
- How can you just decide...

1275
00:55:33,464 --> 00:55:34,364
that my values are hypocritical?

1276
00:55:34,365 --> 00:55:35,599
I keep asking what you think,

1277
00:55:35,600 --> 00:55:37,702
but why do you keep talking...

1278
00:55:37,703 --> 00:55:39,738
about different hypothetical situations...

1279
00:55:39,739 --> 00:55:41,438
instead of the one I gave you?

1280
00:55:41,439 --> 00:55:43,974
I'm asking what your actual values are.

1281
00:55:43,975 --> 00:55:45,142
But I already gave you my answer.

1282
00:55:45,143 --> 00:55:46,877
I don't want to put different values
on people's lives,

1283
00:55:46,878 --> 00:55:48,645
and I don't want to answer questions
like that.

1284
00:55:49,214 --> 00:55:51,815
If you really care about the well-being
of the community,

1285
00:55:52,050 --> 00:55:54,885
these are the kinds of things
you need to think about ahead of time.

1286
00:55:54,886 --> 00:55:57,721
I'm not going to spend money...

1287
00:55:57,722 --> 00:56:00,057
just because something looks good
for the community.

1288
00:56:00,058 --> 00:56:01,392
The idea...

1289
00:56:01,393 --> 00:56:03,394
of something simply "looking good" is
an illusion.

1290
00:56:03,895 --> 00:56:05,696
I don't want everyone in this community...

1291
00:56:05,697 --> 00:56:07,831
to feel pressured to go along
with something...

1292
00:56:08,166 --> 00:56:10,567
just because it looks good on the surface.

1293
00:56:14,239 --> 00:56:16,273
Thank you. And with that,

1294
00:56:16,274 --> 00:56:18,809
we'll wrap up the second round
of the debate.

1295
00:56:18,810 --> 00:56:21,679
Let's take some questions for Superman.

1296
00:56:22,413 --> 00:56:24,414
Benjamin, here's the mic.

1297
00:56:26,017 --> 00:56:27,851
This is not a country,

1298
00:56:28,286 --> 00:56:30,087
and I don't remember the exact wording,

1299
00:56:30,088 --> 00:56:32,256
but in the US Constitution,
there's a line like this.

1300
00:56:33,076 --> 00:56:36,393
"People shouldn't be deprived..."

1301
00:56:36,494 --> 00:56:40,730
"of their life, liberty,
or property interests..."

1302
00:56:41,233 --> 00:56:43,600
"without due process of law."

1303
00:56:43,601 --> 00:56:47,371
If you're going to take someone's life,
there has to be a legal basis for it.

1304
00:56:47,372 --> 00:56:48,639
I don't really know
the Korean Constitution well,

1305
00:56:48,640 --> 00:56:51,108
but I'm sure Korea has something similar.

1306
00:56:51,309 --> 00:56:53,377
The government can't just take away
your property...

1307
00:56:53,378 --> 00:56:56,213
or kill you whenever it feels like it.

1308
00:56:56,214 --> 00:56:58,215
The president can't just do
whatever they want.

1309
00:56:58,216 --> 00:57:00,550
If you're going to kill the person
with 1,000 dollars...

1310
00:57:00,551 --> 00:57:02,953
among those with 5,000 dollars,
3,000 dollars, or 1,000 dollars,

1311
00:57:02,954 --> 00:57:05,923
there needs to be due process.

1312
00:57:06,357 --> 00:57:08,491
So my question is...

1313
00:57:08,492 --> 00:57:11,928
can you actually establish due process
in advance for a situation like this?

1314
00:57:11,929 --> 00:57:13,596
You could decide by majority vote,

1315
00:57:13,597 --> 00:57:16,199
or if you have a strong leader,

1316
00:57:16,200 --> 00:57:18,335
you could just leave it
to their discretion.

1317
00:57:18,603 --> 00:57:21,271
I wanted to know where you stand on that.

1318
00:57:21,638 --> 00:57:24,240
There's actually a similar concept
in the Korean Constitution.

1319
00:57:24,241 --> 00:57:27,077
It's called the principle
of proportionality.

1320
00:57:27,078 --> 00:57:30,580
If the government or a community
wants to restrict basic rights,

1321
00:57:30,881 --> 00:57:33,517
there are limits on how far
those restrictions can go...

1322
00:57:33,518 --> 00:57:34,951
and to what degree.

1323
00:57:34,952 --> 00:57:36,620
You brought up the US Constitution.

1324
00:57:36,621 --> 00:57:39,155
There are probably clear precedents
we could look at,

1325
00:57:39,156 --> 00:57:40,775
so we could draw on principles...

1326
00:57:40,799 --> 00:57:42,159
from both Korean
and US constitutional law...

1327
00:57:42,160 --> 00:57:44,195
and apply them here
to establish a proper process.

1328
00:57:44,562 --> 00:57:46,896
The question I asked him
was actually based...

1329
00:57:46,897 --> 00:57:48,264
on the Fifth Amendment
to the US Constitution.

1330
00:57:48,265 --> 00:57:50,900
The famous Due Process Clause.

1331
00:57:51,135 --> 00:57:53,170
In Article 37, Section 2
of the Korean Constitution,

1332
00:57:53,171 --> 00:57:55,238
it's called the principle
of statutory reservation.

1333
00:57:55,239 --> 00:57:58,908
"If you want to restrict someone's rights,
there must be a legal basis."

1334
00:57:59,277 --> 00:58:02,145
But he brought up the principle
of proportionality instead.

1335
00:58:02,146 --> 00:58:03,380
That's simply incorrect.

1336
00:58:03,682 --> 00:58:06,816
I wanted to test whether he actually
had a governing philosophy...

1337
00:58:06,817 --> 00:58:09,419
and understood what leadership entails,

1338
00:58:09,420 --> 00:58:10,920
and he gave me the wrong answer.

1339
00:58:10,921 --> 00:58:12,021
I found that fascinating.

1340
00:58:12,022 --> 00:58:14,791
I asked him about statutory reservation,

1341
00:58:14,792 --> 00:58:18,028
and his response was essentially,
"I won't restrict them too much."

1342
00:58:18,029 --> 00:58:19,062
That makes no sense.

1343
00:58:19,063 --> 00:58:20,930
I've picked up on that pattern with him...

1344
00:58:20,931 --> 00:58:24,267
throughout the debates
and just from being around him here.

1345
00:58:24,268 --> 00:58:26,437
So as far as I'm concerned,
he got it wrong.

1346
00:58:27,548 --> 00:58:32,552
That concludes the debate,
covering both Rounds 1 and 2.

1347
00:58:32,577 --> 00:58:34,812
You both did a great job.
Let's give them a round of applause.

1348
00:58:35,546 --> 00:58:38,548
Thank you.

1349
00:58:39,684 --> 00:58:42,152
What really stood out to me
about Superman was...

1350
00:58:42,153 --> 00:58:43,954
how bluntly he put it:

1351
00:58:43,955 --> 00:58:46,390
"I won't pressure you all
to act like hypocrites."

1352
00:58:46,391 --> 00:58:48,668
"If you don't want to take the heat,
I'll take it for you."

1353
00:58:48,693 --> 00:58:50,994
A lot of people were probably thinking
the same thing deep down,

1354
00:58:50,995 --> 00:58:52,797
but he had the guts to actually
say it out loud.

1355
00:58:53,197 --> 00:58:56,132
My circumstances in here are completely
different from my life outside,

1356
00:58:56,133 --> 00:59:00,837
so I found myself being swayed
by arguments I would never agree with...

1357
00:59:00,838 --> 00:59:02,505
in the real world.

1358
00:59:03,074 --> 00:59:05,442
In an ideal world, I'd want everyone
to make good money...

1359
00:59:05,443 --> 00:59:07,944
while also making sure
everyone stays safe.

1360
00:59:07,945 --> 00:59:11,148
But we all know
you can't have it both ways,

1361
00:59:11,149 --> 00:59:13,917
so I didn't want to make empty promises.

1362
00:59:13,918 --> 00:59:17,088
I just tried to be transparent
about what I actually believe.

1363
00:59:17,321 --> 00:59:19,589
I'm not sure if my message
really got across.

1364
00:59:19,590 --> 00:59:24,127
I felt an obligation to lay out
my core values and philosophy...

1365
00:59:24,128 --> 00:59:26,195
during the debate.

1366
00:59:26,531 --> 00:59:29,099
I stood my ground pretty aggressively,
to the point...

1367
00:59:29,100 --> 00:59:32,536
where people probably thought
I was just being stubborn.

1368
00:59:32,637 --> 00:59:35,038
I didn't mean to come on so strong.

1369
00:59:35,039 --> 00:59:36,707
I'm completely open to changing course...

1370
00:59:36,708 --> 00:59:39,643
if someone has a better idea
or knows more than I do.

1371
00:59:39,644 --> 00:59:41,878
I'm always willing to listen.

1372
00:59:41,879 --> 00:59:44,982
I just didn't get the chance to say that
during the debate.

1373
00:59:51,322 --> 00:59:52,689
Ten and...

1374
00:59:54,158 --> 00:59:55,125
seven.

1375
00:59:58,262 --> 01:00:00,296
I can't be sure.

1376
01:00:03,901 --> 01:00:07,437
What if I just went rogue
and played for myself?

1377
01:00:08,639 --> 01:00:10,173
What should I do?

1378
01:00:11,676 --> 01:00:13,677
I really want to pull the trigger.
What do I do?

1379
01:00:15,348 --> 01:00:17,448
Should I do it now?

1380
01:00:17,783 --> 01:00:21,819
I'm 95 percent sure,
but there's that 5 percent doubt.

1381
01:00:21,820 --> 01:00:23,954
If my read is wrong,

1382
01:00:23,979 --> 01:00:27,882
I lose 4,000 dollars
and get branded as the spy.

1383
01:00:30,229 --> 01:00:32,663
The ideology check
and favorability vote...

1384
01:00:32,664 --> 01:00:34,631
will now begin.

1385
01:00:35,299 --> 01:00:38,435
Please gather in the main hall.

1386
01:00:38,436 --> 01:00:40,036
- Same as yesterday?
- Are we doing it just like yesterday?

1387
01:00:40,037 --> 01:00:41,838
Everyone good with that?

1388
01:00:41,839 --> 01:00:42,906
- Yes.
- Yes.

1389
01:00:44,016 --> 01:00:48,319
(Like yesterday, the favorability vote
will follow the Panopticon format.)

1390
01:00:55,686 --> 01:01:00,023
(The favorability vote begins.)

1391
01:01:02,426 --> 01:01:04,327
First up, Superman.

1392
01:01:12,303 --> 01:01:14,037
(Vote for three residents you like,
excluding yourself.)

1393
01:01:28,119 --> 01:01:30,454
Nangja. Dark Knight.

1394
01:01:32,890 --> 01:01:34,858
No real reason. It's just random.

1395
01:01:36,727 --> 01:01:39,028
Select a resident to inspect.

1396
01:01:39,430 --> 01:01:41,431
The resident I want to inspect is...

1397
01:01:42,133 --> 01:01:43,767
Superman.

1398
01:01:44,268 --> 01:01:46,703
Everyone is so curious about you.

1399
01:01:46,704 --> 01:01:50,106
We feel like we barely know you.

1400
01:01:50,107 --> 01:01:53,009
(Superman grows suspicious of Benjamin.)

1401
01:01:53,010 --> 01:01:55,211
I checked Superman's code today...

1402
01:01:55,313 --> 01:01:57,148
because of how he botched that question.

1403
01:02:00,818 --> 01:02:05,789
(Superman)

1404
01:02:08,025 --> 01:02:09,792
Thank you!

1405
01:02:29,046 --> 01:02:30,546
Everyone holding up okay?

1406
01:02:32,583 --> 01:02:34,484
Everyone safe, right?

1407
01:02:34,785 --> 01:02:35,753
All right!

1408
01:02:41,692 --> 01:02:47,030
(The 13th resident,
Banu's first favorability vote)

1409
01:02:48,966 --> 01:02:51,668
We're glad to have you here, Banu.

1410
01:02:52,169 --> 01:02:54,036
- Welcome to the Community House.
- Thank you.

1411
01:02:54,037 --> 01:02:55,671
In the Community House,

1412
01:02:55,672 --> 01:02:58,107
residents cast votes each night
for people they favor,

1413
01:02:58,108 --> 01:03:01,177
which accumulate
into an overall favorability score.

1414
01:03:01,812 --> 01:03:04,147
Moving forward,

1415
01:03:04,515 --> 01:03:07,984
that cumulative score
will carry significant weight.

1416
01:03:08,653 --> 01:03:11,488
Because you currently have zero points,

1417
01:03:11,689 --> 01:03:14,057
you are granted two options.

1418
01:03:14,892 --> 01:03:17,560
You may either duplicate...

1419
01:03:17,695 --> 01:03:20,563
another resident's favorability score...

1420
01:03:20,798 --> 01:03:23,199
or siphon half of their points.

1421
01:03:25,903 --> 01:03:27,904
If you choose to copy someone's score,

1422
01:03:28,139 --> 01:03:30,741
that resident will receive
three bonus points.

1423
01:03:33,344 --> 01:03:37,747
(She's likely to copy
Ted or Hippo's score...)

1424
01:03:37,748 --> 01:03:41,017
(since they made her feel welcome.)

1425
01:03:41,185 --> 01:03:43,420
If you choose to steal
half of someone's score,

1426
01:03:43,587 --> 01:03:46,455
their total points will be
slashed in half.

1427
01:03:48,392 --> 01:03:50,628
You'll be receiving 6,000 dollars.

1428
01:03:50,629 --> 01:03:52,562
That money didn't fall out of thin air.

1429
01:03:52,563 --> 01:03:54,686
I just wanted to make sure you knew...

1430
01:03:54,710 --> 01:03:56,666
it came straight out
of our shared savings.

1431
01:03:57,301 --> 01:04:01,404
(Superman draws a hard line with her.)

1432
01:04:04,642 --> 01:04:07,777
(Favorability Points: Copy or Steal)

1433
01:04:34,438 --> 01:04:38,641
The new resident has copied
a resident's favorability score.

1434
01:04:38,642 --> 01:04:39,643
Wait,

1435
01:04:40,479 --> 01:04:41,344
what?

1436
01:04:41,879 --> 01:04:43,546
What's going on?

1437
01:04:43,547 --> 01:04:45,982
- What is this?
- What happened?

1438
01:04:46,183 --> 01:04:48,718
- What does that mean?
- The new resident copied a score.

1439
01:04:48,719 --> 01:04:50,386
What does that even mean?

1440
01:04:50,654 --> 01:04:52,888
- Should we meet up?
- Shouldn't we head downstairs?

1441
01:04:52,889 --> 01:04:53,957
- Head down.
- Let's meet up.

1442
01:04:53,958 --> 01:04:55,958
- Let's talk.
- Head downstairs.

1443
01:05:03,467 --> 01:05:05,268
Today, the spy inspected...

1444
01:05:05,269 --> 01:05:08,571
one resident's complete ideology code.

1445
01:05:09,673 --> 01:05:13,609
Wait, why are they only
announcing that now?

1446
01:05:13,911 --> 01:05:16,579
Sounds like they're
checking one every day.

1447
01:05:16,580 --> 01:05:18,147
Yeah, they're looking at one a day.

1448
01:05:21,118 --> 01:05:22,218
Banu.

1449
01:05:22,219 --> 01:05:26,155
It said you copied a favorability score.
What was that about?

1450
01:05:26,156 --> 01:05:27,758
Did they announce that?

1451
01:05:28,193 --> 01:05:31,828
It told me I could pick someone...

1452
01:05:31,829 --> 01:05:34,264
and copy their favorability score.

1453
01:05:34,265 --> 01:05:35,698
Because it's your first day?

1454
01:05:35,699 --> 01:05:37,734
- Did you get to see their scores?
- No.

1455
01:05:37,735 --> 01:05:40,703
- Just blindly?
- Yes.

1456
01:05:41,338 --> 01:05:44,307
I remember that if I chose
to steal points instead,

1457
01:05:44,508 --> 01:05:49,312
their score would be cut in half.

1458
01:05:49,413 --> 01:05:50,780
Something along those lines.

1459
01:05:50,781 --> 01:05:53,116
It sounds like favorability scores
are going to matter.

1460
01:05:53,117 --> 01:05:54,650
It sounds critical.

1461
01:05:54,651 --> 01:05:56,920
It's also telling...

1462
01:05:56,921 --> 01:06:01,191
that they even gave her the option
to steal someone's score.

1463
01:06:01,192 --> 01:06:03,928
- Who did you pick? Actually, never mind.
- Keep that to yourself.

1464
01:06:05,830 --> 01:06:08,632
(She chose "copy.")

1465
01:06:10,167 --> 01:06:12,034
(Banu chose Ted.)

1466
01:06:12,269 --> 01:06:15,171
You have copied Ted's favorability score.

1467
01:06:16,340 --> 01:06:19,876
(Copy)

1468
01:06:20,611 --> 01:06:23,313
I voted for Banu as a welcoming gesture.

1469
01:06:23,314 --> 01:06:26,750
I gave Banu a heart to welcome her in.

1470
01:06:26,951 --> 01:06:29,686
(All residents voted for Banu.)

1471
01:06:29,687 --> 01:06:31,821
(Day Five Favorability Votes)

1472
01:06:31,822 --> 01:06:32,822
(Banu copied Ted's favorability score.)

1473
01:06:41,932 --> 01:06:43,966
We have 16 minutes left.
Let's put our heads together.

1474
01:06:43,967 --> 01:06:45,044
We need to formulate our questions.

1475
01:06:45,069 --> 01:06:46,870
Here's what you wrote down for me earlier.

1476
01:06:46,871 --> 01:06:48,871
Since we're allowed two questions,

1477
01:06:48,872 --> 01:06:51,240
we have leeway for up to six rejections.

1478
01:06:51,408 --> 01:06:53,444
Let's come up
with two more backup questions.

1479
01:06:53,644 --> 01:06:55,178
Can we ask about the favorability vote?

1480
01:06:55,179 --> 01:06:57,380
- That's good.
- How it will come into play.

1481
01:06:57,381 --> 01:06:58,782
How will it be used?

1482
01:06:58,783 --> 01:07:00,383
I also heard...

1483
01:07:00,384 --> 01:07:03,453
that favorability scores
will be crucial down the line.

1484
01:07:03,888 --> 01:07:05,221
That's what I heard too.

1485
01:07:05,222 --> 01:07:08,124
"What is the purpose
of the cumulative favorability score?"

1486
01:07:08,125 --> 01:07:09,625
I'm curious about that too.

1487
01:07:09,626 --> 01:07:11,193
They didn't announce...

1488
01:07:11,194 --> 01:07:13,496
that the spy could view codes
around dinner.

1489
01:07:13,497 --> 01:07:15,331
I figured they passed on it
until the announcement came on.

1490
01:07:15,332 --> 01:07:17,900
If they're still checking our codes,

1491
01:07:17,901 --> 01:07:20,403
it means holding that intel
offers a massive edge...

1492
01:07:20,404 --> 01:07:22,638
- after Day Six.
- Exactly.

1493
01:07:22,874 --> 01:07:25,341
Does knowing the codes
still hold value later in the game?

1494
01:07:25,342 --> 01:07:27,577
Even when the ideology check
period wraps up,

1495
01:07:27,578 --> 01:07:30,947
- Can the codes...
- still be used to eliminate people?

1496
01:07:30,948 --> 01:07:34,183
I just need to get in there
before the window shuts.

1497
01:07:34,184 --> 01:07:35,251
You'd better get moving.

1498
01:07:35,252 --> 01:07:36,419
Go, now!

1499
01:07:36,420 --> 01:07:38,922
- What about the order?
- Just go right now.

1500
01:07:43,093 --> 01:07:45,728
The Reporter information inquiry
will now begin.

1501
01:07:52,503 --> 01:07:54,704
Due to increased currency circulation
and population growth,

1502
01:07:54,705 --> 01:07:57,273
prices at the Community Mart
have been adjusted.

1503
01:07:57,274 --> 01:07:59,876
- Prices jumped like crazy.
- That's ridiculous.

1504
01:07:59,877 --> 01:08:01,512
Why did they spike so much?

1505
01:08:01,513 --> 01:08:02,946
- It's triple now.
- At least they added beef.

1506
01:08:02,947 --> 01:08:04,747
A Korean beef set is 5,000 dollars.

1507
01:08:04,882 --> 01:08:06,258
No way we're having Korean beef tonight.

1508
01:08:06,283 --> 01:08:08,952
Everything literally tripled.

1509
01:08:08,953 --> 01:08:11,754
We should've stockpiled
meal kits and groceries.

1510
01:08:11,755 --> 01:08:14,123
- We totally should have!
- Eggs and bread, especially.

1511
01:08:16,961 --> 01:08:18,061
This is insane.

1512
01:08:18,062 --> 01:08:19,864
Are they trying to starve us out?

1513
01:08:19,865 --> 01:08:21,298
Everything costs three times as much now.

1514
01:08:21,464 --> 01:08:24,067
It was already nuts
when pork belly was 1,000 dollars,

1515
01:08:24,068 --> 01:08:25,468
and now it's 3,000.

1516
01:08:26,103 --> 01:08:27,437
I checked the price of soju.

1517
01:08:27,904 --> 01:08:29,839
The second I saw it,
I ran straight to check the fridge.

1518
01:08:29,840 --> 01:08:31,474
We've got our drinking crew.

1519
01:08:31,475 --> 01:08:34,109
There was exactly one bottle
left in the fridge.

1520
01:08:34,445 --> 01:08:37,380
With no guarantee on future earnings,

1521
01:08:37,449 --> 01:08:39,616
we really need to tighten our belts.

1522
01:08:39,617 --> 01:08:41,818
The Reporter is back.

1523
01:08:41,919 --> 01:08:43,453
- Is everyone assembled?
- Yes.

1524
01:08:44,254 --> 01:08:46,565
"Can the inspected codes
still be utilized..."

1525
01:08:46,590 --> 01:08:48,624
"after the ideology check period ends?"

1526
01:08:48,625 --> 01:08:49,558
(Nangja debriefs the residents.)

1527
01:08:51,037 --> 01:08:54,306
Will our codes still be valid...

1528
01:08:54,331 --> 01:08:57,900
once the ideology check period concludes?

1529
01:08:59,636 --> 01:09:01,370
No, they won't.

1530
01:09:03,807 --> 01:09:04,974
They won't be.

1531
01:09:06,710 --> 01:09:08,077
So we only have one day left
to worry about.

1532
01:09:08,078 --> 01:09:09,379
There's no point
in gathering any more codes.

1533
01:09:09,380 --> 01:09:11,280
We just need to hold out for one more day.

1534
01:09:11,307 --> 01:09:11,883
Next, please.

1535
01:09:11,908 --> 01:09:14,737
"What is the purpose
of the cumulative favorability score?"

1536
01:09:15,519 --> 01:09:18,354
What is the purpose
of the cumulative favorability score?

1537
01:09:18,756 --> 01:09:20,756
Please stand by.

1538
01:09:23,694 --> 01:09:27,396
"It may later serve as a criterion
to determine resident eliminations."

1539
01:09:28,165 --> 01:09:30,166
- That's huge.
- That's massive.

1540
01:09:30,167 --> 01:09:32,335
Favorability scores could decide
who gets eliminated.

1541
01:09:32,336 --> 01:09:34,371
Does that mean whoever
has the lowest score is gone?

1542
01:09:34,738 --> 01:09:37,907
But if you have enough cash,
you might still buy your way out.

1543
01:09:38,108 --> 01:09:40,943
There will be two competing variables.

1544
01:09:40,944 --> 01:09:43,088
One point might be worth
around 1,000 dollars.

1545
01:09:43,113 --> 01:09:46,015
People might even start trading
favorability points.

1546
01:09:46,040 --> 01:09:48,608
My score is in the gutter. I'm screwed.

1547
01:09:56,560 --> 01:09:58,261
I only got a single point today.

1548
01:09:58,395 --> 01:10:01,497
What do I do? This looks really bad.

1549
01:10:01,498 --> 01:10:04,500
(Day Five Favorability Points:
Go Ae Shin, 1)

1550
01:10:04,501 --> 01:10:07,169
(Day Five Favorability Points:
Polar Bear, 3)

1551
01:10:07,170 --> 01:10:09,405
(Day Five Favorability Points: Ted, 5)

1552
01:10:09,406 --> 01:10:12,608
(Day Five Favorability Points: Hippo, 2)

1553
01:10:12,609 --> 01:10:14,744
(Day Five Favorability Points: Banu, 20)

1554
01:10:14,745 --> 01:10:16,879
(Day Five Favorability Points: Nangja, 2)

1555
01:10:16,880 --> 01:10:19,382
(Day Five Favorability Points:
Superman, 5)

1556
01:10:19,383 --> 01:10:26,889
(Day Five Favorability Vote)

1557
01:10:29,960 --> 01:10:32,395
(Day Six, morning)

1558
01:10:32,396 --> 01:10:35,831
Today is the day
you elect the Lifetime Leader.

1559
01:10:36,733 --> 01:10:38,600
The two candidates,

1560
01:10:39,336 --> 01:10:42,004
please prepare your final speeches
before the vote.

1561
01:10:46,410 --> 01:10:47,910
"Dear residents,"

1562
01:10:47,911 --> 01:10:51,381
"I'm Polar Bear, and I'm running
for Lifetime Leader."

1563
01:10:51,682 --> 01:10:53,116
(Superman)

1564
01:11:12,903 --> 01:11:14,404
I'm a little nervous.

1565
01:11:14,815 --> 01:11:18,533
What should I say to move these people?

1566
01:11:18,558 --> 01:11:19,609
That's what's been on my mind.

1567
01:11:19,610 --> 01:11:21,944
How can I earn their trust?

1568
01:11:22,413 --> 01:11:24,448
I've been thinking a lot about my speech.

1569
01:11:28,519 --> 01:11:31,087
Have you been up on the podium yet?

1570
01:11:31,621 --> 01:11:32,656
I just took a look.

1571
01:11:33,690 --> 01:11:35,457
You spoke really well.

1572
01:11:35,592 --> 01:11:38,894
I can't even remember
how I did in yesterday's debate.

1573
01:11:39,062 --> 01:11:41,563
You kept bringing up immigrants,

1574
01:11:41,564 --> 01:11:44,400
even knowing how sensitive that topic is.

1575
01:11:44,401 --> 01:11:47,270
To be honest, I couldn't say it out loud,

1576
01:11:47,271 --> 01:11:48,837
but I agreed with you on a lot of points.

1577
01:11:48,838 --> 01:11:50,372
One side sounded great in theory
and very idealistic,

1578
01:11:50,707 --> 01:11:53,677
but I questioned
how realistic it actually was.

1579
01:11:53,678 --> 01:11:55,745
Dark Knight and I kept talking about that.

1580
01:11:56,186 --> 01:11:58,855
In terms of practicality,
Polar Bear is at a disadvantage.

1581
01:12:00,284 --> 01:12:04,621
It bothered me all day yesterday...

1582
01:12:04,954 --> 01:12:06,889
because it went against my core values.

1583
01:12:06,890 --> 01:12:09,091
I even thought about this.

1584
01:12:09,193 --> 01:12:10,960
If it had been cash on the line,
we wouldn't have made that call.

1585
01:12:10,985 --> 01:12:12,219
- Naturally.
- Hello.

1586
01:12:12,262 --> 01:12:13,395
Hello.

1587
01:12:15,566 --> 01:12:17,534
Did you hear about the pu-erh tea?

1588
01:12:17,535 --> 01:12:18,433
No.

1589
01:12:18,434 --> 01:12:19,702
I just came downstairs.

1590
01:12:27,644 --> 01:12:30,145
I felt bad...

1591
01:12:30,514 --> 01:12:32,849
because I brought you up
so much yesterday.

1592
01:12:33,450 --> 01:12:35,684
I would've been upset...

1593
01:12:36,453 --> 01:12:37,654
if I were in your shoes.

1594
01:12:37,788 --> 01:12:40,590
It left me feeling a bit guilty.

1595
01:12:40,591 --> 01:12:43,426
I'm a fairly logical person,

1596
01:12:43,427 --> 01:12:45,729
so I didn't take it personally.

1597
01:12:46,230 --> 01:12:47,464
Don't worry about it at all.

1598
01:12:49,566 --> 01:12:51,500
- Good morning.
- Morning.

1599
01:12:56,073 --> 01:12:57,240
(The announcement will now begin.)

1600
01:12:57,241 --> 01:13:00,109
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

1601
01:13:00,744 --> 01:13:04,580
Today, we elect the Lifetime Leader
who will guide the Community House...

1602
01:13:04,782 --> 01:13:07,115
for the remainder of our stay.

1603
01:13:07,651 --> 01:13:10,295
In 30 minutes, the two candidates
will deliver their final speeches...

1604
01:13:10,320 --> 01:13:12,488
- at the central plaza.
- Thirty minutes?

1605
01:13:12,489 --> 01:13:14,189
- We only have 30 minutes?
- That's insane.

1606
01:13:15,225 --> 01:13:17,660
- Did you finish writing your speech?
- Not yet.

1607
01:13:18,762 --> 01:13:21,197
When we gathered this morning,

1608
01:13:21,632 --> 01:13:24,000
I noticed quite a few people...

1609
01:13:24,001 --> 01:13:25,603
leaning toward a more
conservative candidate right now.

1610
01:13:25,604 --> 01:13:26,502
I can tell.

1611
01:13:26,846 --> 01:13:29,422
People are having a hard time
buying into the idea...

1612
01:13:29,447 --> 01:13:31,137
that you can bail them out...

1613
01:13:31,161 --> 01:13:32,809
even if they hit rock bottom
with zero dollars.

1614
01:13:36,647 --> 01:13:40,883
The more unstable things get around here,

1615
01:13:40,884 --> 01:13:43,619
the more inclined people will be...

1616
01:13:43,644 --> 01:13:46,913
to want a conservative leader.

1617
01:13:47,233 --> 01:13:49,168
I'm fully backing Polar Bear,

1618
01:13:49,193 --> 01:13:51,894
so I've basically been helping her
shape her policies...

1619
01:13:51,895 --> 01:13:54,196
and offering strategic advice.

1620
01:13:54,197 --> 01:13:56,665
I've also been keeping her in the loop...

1621
01:13:56,900 --> 01:13:59,735
on what everyone is saying...

1622
01:13:59,736 --> 01:14:02,638
and the overall mood of the room.

1623
01:14:03,106 --> 01:14:05,240
We should head to the central plaza.

1624
01:14:08,712 --> 01:14:09,980
This feels wonderful.

1625
01:14:10,147 --> 01:14:12,015
- The weather is gorgeous.
- Soaking up that vitamin D.

1626
01:14:12,749 --> 01:14:14,785
Ideal weather for listening to speeches.

1627
01:14:16,553 --> 01:14:17,687
Even I'm feeling the nerves.

1628
01:14:19,489 --> 01:14:23,525
(Lifetime Leader candidates:
Polar Bear and Superman)

1629
01:14:30,167 --> 01:14:33,503
The first candidate, Polar Bear,
will now deliver her address.

1630
01:14:35,106 --> 01:14:40,643
The 15-minute speech begins now.

1631
01:14:42,012 --> 01:14:44,780
When I first ran for leader,

1632
01:14:44,781 --> 01:14:48,517
I thought it hardly mattered who won.

1633
01:14:48,518 --> 01:14:52,088
I assumed Superman and I would simply
govern in our own separate ways.

1634
01:14:52,089 --> 01:14:53,522
But after that debate,

1635
01:14:53,890 --> 01:14:56,324
I realized how fundamentally
different our mindsets are.

1636
01:14:56,325 --> 01:14:57,870
I found myself completely disagreeing...

1637
01:14:57,895 --> 01:14:59,795
with the way he intends
to run this community.

1638
01:14:59,796 --> 01:15:03,966
It made me feel strongly
that I had to step in and stop him.

1639
01:15:03,967 --> 01:15:07,002
I'm going to give this
everything I have to beat him.

1640
01:15:08,406 --> 01:15:09,539
Hello, everyone.

1641
01:15:09,540 --> 01:15:12,708
I'm Polar Bear, and I'm running
to be your Lifetime Leader.

1642
01:15:12,709 --> 01:15:13,777
It's wonderful to see you all.

1643
01:15:14,378 --> 01:15:15,678
Polar Bear!

1644
01:15:17,982 --> 01:15:22,118
We're currently in a bind...

1645
01:15:22,189 --> 01:15:24,910
where our individual self-interests
and collective survival are at odds.

1646
01:15:25,057 --> 01:15:27,857
Now that the spy poses an imminent threat,

1647
01:15:27,858 --> 01:15:30,326
no matter how much money we pile up,

1648
01:15:30,327 --> 01:15:33,295
any one of us could still be eliminated...

1649
01:15:33,296 --> 01:15:34,530
at any given moment.

1650
01:15:34,731 --> 01:15:37,416
That is precisely why we must unite...

1651
01:15:37,441 --> 01:15:40,836
and leverage our collective intelligence
to expose the spy.

1652
01:15:41,237 --> 01:15:44,073
As a proactive step toward our survival,

1653
01:15:44,074 --> 01:15:48,144
I pledge to establish
a dedicated community command center...

1654
01:15:48,145 --> 01:15:49,578
focused solely on unmasking the spy.

1655
01:15:50,046 --> 01:15:52,382
Furthermore, I want to ensure...

1656
01:15:52,496 --> 01:15:55,619
that being broke or holding
a low favorability score...

1657
01:15:55,620 --> 01:15:58,354
doesn't automatically
punch your ticket out of here.

1658
01:15:58,355 --> 01:16:01,791
I will build a social safety net
so that those disparities...

1659
01:16:01,792 --> 01:16:03,960
never dictate who lives and who dies.

1660
01:16:03,961 --> 01:16:05,261
(Ensuring everyone has
a chance to survive)

1661
01:16:05,662 --> 01:16:08,143
Hypocrisy came up quite a bit
during yesterday's debate,

1662
01:16:08,168 --> 01:16:09,633
and I gave it a lot of thought afterward.

1663
01:16:10,534 --> 01:16:14,003
Is it genuinely hypocritical
to want to keep everyone safe...

1664
01:16:14,004 --> 01:16:15,671
and see all of us survive?

1665
01:16:16,239 --> 01:16:18,707
Am I misleading you
by striving for something...

1666
01:16:18,708 --> 01:16:20,277
that seems out of reach?

1667
01:16:21,244 --> 01:16:24,379
The more I reflected on it,
the more I wondered,

1668
01:16:24,648 --> 01:16:26,048
why must it be impossible?

1669
01:16:26,383 --> 01:16:29,218
Why do we automatically assume
that everyone only acts...

1670
01:16:29,219 --> 01:16:30,753
out of pure self-interest?

1671
01:16:30,887 --> 01:16:32,197
I know for a fact some of us don't.

1672
01:16:32,222 --> 01:16:35,292
Why should we write off genuine
good intentions as mere hypocrisy?

1673
01:16:37,027 --> 01:16:38,895
I refuse to see that as hypocrisy.

1674
01:16:39,095 --> 01:16:41,630
I don't want us to throw in the towel
on each other.

1675
01:16:41,765 --> 01:16:44,776
I want to establish a safety net
for every single one of you...

1676
01:16:44,801 --> 01:16:46,402
and foster a community...

1677
01:16:46,403 --> 01:16:48,270
where we genuinely
look out for one another.

1678
01:16:48,572 --> 01:16:52,542
I welcome your guidance
and insight moving forward.

1679
01:16:52,543 --> 01:16:53,476
Thank you.

1680
01:16:58,682 --> 01:17:01,384
Polar Bear came across
far more confident...

1681
01:17:01,385 --> 01:17:03,152
than she did the previous day.

1682
01:17:03,153 --> 01:17:05,755
Right out of the gate,

1683
01:17:05,756 --> 01:17:09,225
she laid out a coherent vision
and concrete plans for the house.

1684
01:17:09,226 --> 01:17:10,626
"I can turn this into reality."

1685
01:17:10,795 --> 01:17:13,196
She had clearly thought
everything through...

1686
01:17:13,197 --> 01:17:15,064
and had far more answers this time around.

1687
01:17:15,065 --> 01:17:16,055
Polar Bear's core pitch was,

1688
01:17:16,079 --> 01:17:19,169
"I will make sure no one is
put in harm's way..."

1689
01:17:19,170 --> 01:17:21,938
"and that everyone makes it
through this experiment safely."

1690
01:17:21,939 --> 01:17:23,539
That was the essence of her message.

1691
01:17:23,540 --> 01:17:26,676
It certainly makes me
want to rally behind her.

1692
01:17:26,677 --> 01:17:28,611
But realistically,
can she actually pull that off?

1693
01:17:30,013 --> 01:17:31,413
I truly wish she could.

1694
01:17:31,414 --> 01:17:33,916
"I'll do everything in my power
to ensure nobody gets eliminated."

1695
01:17:33,917 --> 01:17:38,921
To me, that sounds like saying,
"I'm going to invent a miracle cure..."

1696
01:17:38,922 --> 01:17:42,524
"so nobody ever has to die..."

1697
01:17:42,525 --> 01:17:45,728
"and we can all live forever."

1698
01:17:45,729 --> 01:17:48,974
It's far too utopian, far too idealistic,
and frankly detached from reality.

1699
01:17:48,999 --> 01:17:53,002
I've lived long enough to know...

1700
01:17:53,003 --> 01:17:54,838
that such a world simply doesn't exist.

1701
01:17:55,607 --> 01:17:59,075
The second candidate, Superman,
will now deliver his address.

1702
01:18:00,178 --> 01:18:01,711
Let's try something first.

1703
01:18:01,912 --> 01:18:04,381
Turn around and take a look at each other.

1704
01:18:05,215 --> 01:18:06,115
Go ahead.

1705
01:18:07,551 --> 01:18:08,852
How does that make you feel?

1706
01:18:09,286 --> 01:18:10,919
- Cute.
- "Cute."

1707
01:18:11,154 --> 01:18:13,021
- Adorable.
- "Adorable."

1708
01:18:13,790 --> 01:18:15,858
When I look at every single one
of you like this,

1709
01:18:15,859 --> 01:18:18,260
it warms my heart.

1710
01:18:19,195 --> 01:18:21,696
On day one, looking around the room,

1711
01:18:21,898 --> 01:18:23,265
I felt deeply uneasy.

1712
01:18:23,400 --> 01:18:26,068
Some faces looked vaguely familiar,
but I wasn't entirely sure.

1713
01:18:26,069 --> 01:18:27,970
I didn't quite know what to think.

1714
01:18:28,271 --> 01:18:30,272
Now that five days have passed,

1715
01:18:30,273 --> 01:18:33,875
those reservations have
completely melted away.

1716
01:18:34,244 --> 01:18:36,512
I desperately want this community
to reach the finish line intact.

1717
01:18:36,513 --> 01:18:39,782
I get this genuine, warm feeling,

1718
01:18:39,783 --> 01:18:42,551
and my instinct is to protect all of you.

1719
01:18:43,119 --> 01:18:45,955
Yet realistically, how long
can we actually maintain...

1720
01:18:45,956 --> 01:18:47,457
this level of harmony between us?

1721
01:18:47,857 --> 01:18:50,659
We were already pushed
to the brink yesterday,

1722
01:18:50,660 --> 01:18:53,929
and there will be plenty more tests
down the road.

1723
01:18:53,930 --> 01:18:56,332
We have to look that reality
squarely in the face.

1724
01:18:58,068 --> 01:18:59,257
Up to this point,

1725
01:18:59,281 --> 01:19:02,972
we've taken a purely
defensive posture against the spy.

1726
01:19:03,441 --> 01:19:06,074
And we still haven't uncovered
their identity.

1727
01:19:06,475 --> 01:19:09,244
If I am elected leader today,

1728
01:19:09,412 --> 01:19:11,046
instead of sitting back on defense,

1729
01:19:11,047 --> 01:19:13,115
I'll go on the offensive
to hunt down the spy.

1730
01:19:13,116 --> 01:19:14,316
(Taking an aggressive approach
to rooting out the spy)

1731
01:19:14,985 --> 01:19:18,387
My second priority is
internal community friction.

1732
01:19:18,989 --> 01:19:21,357
That will be an uphill battle to manage.

1733
01:19:21,358 --> 01:19:23,826
The spy is an obvious adversary,

1734
01:19:24,294 --> 01:19:27,796
but I also believe there are
individuals here who might...

1735
01:19:27,797 --> 01:19:29,598
turn their backs on the group...

1736
01:19:29,899 --> 01:19:31,667
and act entirely out of selfish greed.

1737
01:19:31,668 --> 01:19:33,335
What executive authority do I hold?

1738
01:19:33,336 --> 01:19:34,571
I possess the power of taxation.

1739
01:19:34,596 --> 01:19:37,607
I will levy punitive taxes on residents
acting purely out of self-interest.

1740
01:19:37,974 --> 01:19:38,940
Rest assured.

1741
01:19:38,941 --> 01:19:40,509
I will guarantee...

1742
01:19:40,644 --> 01:19:42,479
that their actions carry consequences.

1743
01:19:42,879 --> 01:19:43,880
Goodness.

1744
01:19:44,280 --> 01:19:45,914
Earlier in my speech,

1745
01:19:45,915 --> 01:19:48,150
I mentioned feeling
a warm fondness for this group.

1746
01:19:48,752 --> 01:19:50,252
Lately, that feeling
has been overwhelming.

1747
01:19:50,253 --> 01:19:52,288
While trying to figure out
what to say up here today,

1748
01:19:52,455 --> 01:19:54,389
I took a long look at all of you.

1749
01:19:54,390 --> 01:19:56,525
We come from completely
different walks of life,

1750
01:19:56,693 --> 01:19:58,728
holding wildly contrasting perspectives.

1751
01:19:58,995 --> 01:20:01,797
It felt as if 13 distinct universes...

1752
01:20:01,798 --> 01:20:04,199
had suddenly collided
right before my eyes.

1753
01:20:04,200 --> 01:20:05,568
We are so fundamentally different.

1754
01:20:06,169 --> 01:20:09,238
Take Polar Bear.

1755
01:20:09,239 --> 01:20:12,207
She is an exceptional leader,
sharp and level-headed...

1756
01:20:12,232 --> 01:20:15,334
yet deeply compassionate at her core.

1757
01:20:15,554 --> 01:20:19,059
Nangja has served
as our emotional anchor...

1758
01:20:19,084 --> 01:20:20,749
and handles her duties
as Reporter brilliantly.

1759
01:20:20,750 --> 01:20:22,751
Seeing Sugar just instantly
brightens your mood.

1760
01:20:22,752 --> 01:20:24,486
Things might still feel
a bit awkward between us,

1761
01:20:24,487 --> 01:20:26,256
but I genuinely smile
every time I see her.

1762
01:20:27,490 --> 01:20:30,692
Superman normally comes across
as pretty intense,

1763
01:20:30,693 --> 01:20:34,363
yet during his speech,
he had a tailored thought for everyone.

1764
01:20:34,388 --> 01:20:38,524
I felt both candidates
had their own distinct charm.

1765
01:20:38,935 --> 01:20:42,704
His whole speech carried great energy,
and he was witty.

1766
01:20:42,815 --> 01:20:44,616
I loved how he addressed
everyone individually...

1767
01:20:44,641 --> 01:20:46,908
offering personalized observations...

1768
01:20:46,909 --> 01:20:49,978
on what makes each person tick.

1769
01:20:50,280 --> 01:20:51,847
That really resonated with me.

1770
01:20:52,348 --> 01:20:54,716
He framed each person
as their own little universe,

1771
01:20:54,717 --> 01:20:56,552
so going purely by the speeches,

1772
01:20:56,553 --> 01:20:58,486
his was the one that won me over.

1773
01:20:58,822 --> 01:21:01,291
It had a certain Trump-like showmanship.

1774
01:21:02,126 --> 01:21:05,394
Everyone was lined up side by side,

1775
01:21:05,929 --> 01:21:08,797
and standing in front of them,
looking out at the crowd,

1776
01:21:08,798 --> 01:21:11,100
I can still vividly recall
the emotion of that moment.

1777
01:21:11,367 --> 01:21:13,601
Twelve entirely separate universes...

1778
01:21:13,602 --> 01:21:15,737
had abruptly walked into my life.

1779
01:21:18,108 --> 01:21:21,477
It caught me completely off guard.

1780
01:21:22,779 --> 01:21:23,645
Just...

1781
01:21:24,414 --> 01:21:25,514
standing up there...

1782
01:21:26,483 --> 01:21:27,717
looking out at them...

1783
01:21:28,852 --> 01:21:31,687
the memory of that feeling
is still so vivid.

1784
01:21:31,688 --> 01:21:33,155
It gave me chills.

1785
01:21:35,091 --> 01:21:36,392
I really meant every word.

1786
01:21:36,794 --> 01:21:38,193
This is so embarrassing to admit.

1787
01:21:38,194 --> 01:21:41,630
(Superman: right-wing, equalitarian,
upper-middle class, conservative)

1788
01:21:41,631 --> 01:21:42,665
I feel so exposed.

1789
01:21:44,510 --> 01:21:46,578
(Hippo: left-wing, feminist,
working class, progressive)

1790
01:21:46,603 --> 01:21:49,538
Everyone else seemed thoroughly moved,
but it fell completely flat for me.

1791
01:21:49,539 --> 01:21:51,216
To my ears, it sounded
like pure political theater.

1792
01:21:51,241 --> 01:21:53,209
He made a show of going down the line
person by person.

1793
01:21:53,476 --> 01:21:56,111
It's blatantly obvious
when someone takes the time...

1794
01:21:56,112 --> 01:21:58,246
to observe you closely
and offer genuine insights,

1795
01:21:58,515 --> 01:22:03,451
versus when they're just running
through a checklist because they have to.

1796
01:22:03,620 --> 01:22:05,821
He kept waxing poetic about each of us
being our own little cosmos,

1797
01:22:05,822 --> 01:22:07,923
but it completely missed the mark with me.

1798
01:22:08,057 --> 01:22:11,226
Everyone claimed to be deeply touched,

1799
01:22:11,227 --> 01:22:13,663
but I think he was just playing
to the crowd to run out the clock.

1800
01:22:13,797 --> 01:22:15,765
Besides, he barely even acknowledged me.

1801
01:22:16,024 --> 01:22:17,100
And then there's Gray.

1802
01:22:17,367 --> 01:22:19,468
True to his name,

1803
01:22:19,469 --> 01:22:22,604
he has this grounding presence
that instantly puts everyone at ease.

1804
01:22:22,605 --> 01:22:24,740
He brings a genuine sense
of calm and stability to the room.

1805
01:22:24,774 --> 01:22:27,677
He simply had surplus time
after running through his core points.

1806
01:22:28,645 --> 01:22:30,679
I will safeguard
each and every one of you.

1807
01:22:30,680 --> 01:22:34,041
I will shoulder the burdens
of the hardest decisions...

1808
01:22:34,066 --> 01:22:36,885
so you don't have to carry that weight.

1809
01:22:37,220 --> 01:22:39,321
I ask for your vote. Thank you.

1810
01:22:40,423 --> 01:22:41,323
Incredible!

1811
01:22:44,494 --> 01:22:46,495
He framed himself masterfully...

1812
01:22:46,496 --> 01:22:49,264
as a deeply empathetic
and protective leader.

1813
01:22:49,265 --> 01:22:51,868
I realized that's the textbook way
to deliver a political speech.

1814
01:22:52,435 --> 01:22:54,436
"What was I thinking?"

1815
01:22:54,640 --> 01:22:56,591
I was so wrapped up in my own agenda...

1816
01:22:56,623 --> 01:22:59,007
that I failed to truly engage
with the audience in front of me.

1817
01:22:59,008 --> 01:23:00,410
I walked away feeling like I'd lost.

1818
01:23:01,945 --> 01:23:04,113
It felt remarkably similar
to real-world politics.

1819
01:23:04,280 --> 01:23:06,683
These two candidates aren't
all that different fundamentally,

1820
01:23:06,708 --> 01:23:08,059
yet they strongly emphasize
their contrasts.

1821
01:23:08,084 --> 01:23:09,719
On paper, they share broad similarities,

1822
01:23:09,953 --> 01:23:11,855
yet they split over fine margins.

1823
01:23:11,856 --> 01:23:13,789
They even fell along gender lines,

1824
01:23:13,790 --> 01:23:16,592
mirroring a classic two-party dynamic.

1825
01:23:16,593 --> 01:23:17,448
I couldn't care less who wins,

1826
01:23:17,472 --> 01:23:18,736
as long as they don't start
tearing each other apart.

1827
01:23:18,761 --> 01:23:21,863
I just want whichever leader takes over
to keep everyone alive...

1828
01:23:22,031 --> 01:23:23,732
so I can keep stacking prize money.

1829
01:23:23,867 --> 01:23:25,034
I'm indifferent to who takes the title.

1830
01:23:27,304 --> 01:23:31,606
The Lifetime Leader election...

1831
01:23:31,841 --> 01:23:34,943
will commence in two hours.

1832
01:23:35,278 --> 01:23:38,981
This has been an announcement
from the Community Center.

1833
01:23:39,006 --> 01:23:39,939
Well done, everyone.

1834
01:23:41,551 --> 01:23:44,820
(The final speeches have concluded.)

1835
01:23:44,821 --> 01:23:46,955
(Time Remaining
Until the Lifetime Leader Election)

1836
01:23:46,956 --> 01:23:48,624
(Official campaigning is now underway.)

1837
01:23:48,625 --> 01:23:49,658
Hey there.

1838
01:23:49,826 --> 01:23:51,193
- Nangja.
- Yeah?

1839
01:23:51,194 --> 01:23:52,861
- Back me up on this.
- How so?

1840
01:23:52,862 --> 01:23:54,030
By giving me your vote.

1841
01:23:55,298 --> 01:23:56,866
Banu, have you made up your mind?

1842
01:23:57,166 --> 01:23:58,468
I'd appreciate your vote.

1843
01:23:59,836 --> 01:24:02,271
Superman seemed to tailor his message...

1844
01:24:02,272 --> 01:24:04,573
depending on who he was talking to.

1845
01:24:04,574 --> 01:24:07,009
Politicians running for office...

1846
01:24:07,343 --> 01:24:10,045
will say whatever it takes
to secure a win.

1847
01:24:10,046 --> 01:24:11,913
Punitive taxes are a sensible move.

1848
01:24:11,914 --> 01:24:14,316
Like I said, punitive taxation.

1849
01:24:14,317 --> 01:24:15,317
(Superman champions punitive taxation.)

1850
01:24:15,518 --> 01:24:18,155
- Deciding on a candidate is brutal.
- It really is.

1851
01:24:18,921 --> 01:24:21,790
(Lifetime Leader Election)

1852
01:24:23,259 --> 01:24:24,793
(Who will become the Lifetime Leader...)

1853
01:24:24,794 --> 01:24:27,362
(of the Community House?)

1854
01:24:28,031 --> 01:24:30,733
We will now announce the election results.

1855
01:24:32,635 --> 01:24:33,969
The final tally is...

1856
01:24:34,704 --> 01:24:35,671
I'm so nervous.

1857
01:24:37,641 --> 01:24:40,576
Eleven votes...

1858
01:24:42,379 --> 01:24:44,579
to two votes.

1859
01:24:44,580 --> 01:24:45,947
(11 votes vs. 2 votes)

1860
01:24:46,749 --> 01:24:49,217
The new leader
of the Community House is...

1861
01:24:51,287 --> 01:24:52,120
Good grief.

1862
01:24:52,855 --> 01:24:54,022
Oh, wow.

1863
01:24:57,727 --> 01:24:59,295
To determine today's elimination,

1864
01:24:59,296 --> 01:25:01,730
we will proceed
with the code-deduction game.

1865
01:25:01,731 --> 01:25:03,298
Someone has to go home.

1866
01:25:03,299 --> 01:25:05,034
One of us faces
immediate elimination today.

1867
01:25:05,035 --> 01:25:07,269
That means one person is guaranteed
to leave, no matter what.

1868
01:25:07,270 --> 01:25:09,004
- We have to sacrifice one resident.
- Yes.

1869
01:25:09,005 --> 01:25:10,272
They're forcing us to choose...

1870
01:25:10,273 --> 01:25:12,007
between picking
a target collaboratively...

1871
01:25:12,008 --> 01:25:13,543
or tearing each other apart
out of paranoia.

1872
01:25:13,776 --> 01:25:15,276
Reveal the complete code to me.

1873
01:25:15,277 --> 01:25:16,546
Don't drag this out.

1874
01:25:17,146 --> 01:25:19,981
There have been two casualties already.

1875
01:25:19,982 --> 01:25:22,218
People are about to start
dropping like flies.
